# ============================================================
# KEY ECONOMIC PARTNERS — verified creditor/investor/trade relationships.
# Deliberately scoped to cases with solid sourcing rather than attempted for
# all 26 countries — see Methodology tab for the explicit scope note. Framed
# as "economic partners" and "sources of regional tension," not informal
# "allies/enemies" labels, matching how political risk analysts actually
# describe these relationships.
# ============================================================
KEY_ECONOMIC_PARTNERS = {
    'AFG': {'summary': "Afghanistan's central-bank reserves -- roughly $9bn frozen by the US and $2bn by European governments after the August 2021 Taliban takeover -- remain the defining fact of its external financial position, with $3.5bn now held in a Swiss-based, Taliban-independent 'Fund for the Afghan People' established in September 2022 for stabilization purposes only. The United States, despite having no formal diplomatic relationship with the Taliban government, remains by far the largest source of humanitarian funding, accounting for 43.7% of Afghanistan's $1.71bn in 2024 international funding -- a flow that was thrown into crisis by a US aid freeze ordered in January 2025. China has emerged as the most economically engaged outside power, pursuing mining concessions and limited trade, while Pakistan and Iran remain the dominant (if increasingly strained) trade-transit partners. Pakistan is simultaneously Afghanistan's most important historical trade partner and, since late 2025, its most significant adversary, with a border war cutting bilateral trade nearly in half and pushing Afghan exporters toward Iranian and Central Asian routes instead.", 'sources': [('PBS Frontline: Taliban takeover -- how frozen assets, foreign aid impacts Afghanistan', 'https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/frontline/article/taliban-takeover-how-frozen-assets-foreign-aid-impacts-afghanistan'), ("USIP: US to move Afghanistan's frozen central bank reserves to new Swiss fund", 'https://www.usip.org/publications/2022/09/us-move-afghanistans-frozen-central-bank-reserves-new-swiss-fund'), ('Afghan Witness: US aid freeze leaves Afghan economy and aid sector in crisis', 'https://www.info-res.org/afghan-witness/reports/us-aid-freeze-leaves-afghan-economy-and-aid-sector-in-crisis/'), ('Modern Diplomacy: Afghan trade holds steady in 2025 despite Pakistan border closures', 'https://moderndiplomacy.eu/2026/01/06/afghan-trade-holds-steady-in-2025-despite-pakistan-border-closures/')]},
    'ARE': {'summary': "The UAE carries very little sovereign external debt relative to its asset base and is a major net creditor internationally through its sovereign wealth funds -- ADIA, Mubadala, and ADQ -- rather than a borrower from the IMF or World Bank; the IMF conducts only routine Article IV surveillance. China and India are the UAE's largest trading partners, with non-oil trade increasingly driven by CEPA agreements, while the United States remains the top destination for Emirati sovereign investment and the UAE's principal security guarantor. The UAE has become one of the most active outbound investors in distressed and strategic regional assets: ADQ and Mubadala provided the equity core of the $35 billion Ras El-Hekma deal with Egypt in February 2024, converting frozen deposits into a real-estate stake at the height of Egypt's currency crisis. Israel became a significant new economic partner after the 2020 Abraham Accords and 2022 CEPA, though the relationship has been tested by the Gaza war since October 2023. The UAE's removal from the FATF grey list in February 2024, after two years of AML/CFT remediation, reduced friction with Western financial counterparties and reinforced its ambitions as a global financial-services hub.", 'sources': [("The Washington Institute, IMF and UAE swoop in to ease Egypt's economic crisis", 'https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/imf-and-uae-swoop-ease-egypts-economic-crisis'), ('Norton Rose Fulbright, UAE removed from the FATF grey list', 'https://www.nortonrosefulbright.com/en/knowledge/publications/eb06aa7c/uae-removed-from-the-fatf-grey-list'), ('The National, UAE foreign trade hits record Dh3 trillion in 2024 as CEPAs drive growth', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2025/02/05/uae-foreign-trade-hits-record-dh3-trillion-in-2024-as-cepas-drive-growth/'), ('PwC, UAE-Israel CEPA enters into force', 'https://www.pwc.com/m1/en/services/tax/me-tax-legal-news/2023/uae-israel-cepa.html')]},
    'BGD': {'summary': "The European Union and United States are Bangladesh's most important economic partners by far, together absorbing roughly 70% of its ready-made garment exports and making preferential trade access (EU's GSP scheme in particular) central to Bangladesh's export model, a status complicated by the country's scheduled November 2026 graduation from Least Developed Country status. China is the dominant source of imports -- machinery, fabrics and industrial inputs feeding the garment sector -- and a significant infrastructure lender, though its financing role is smaller than in Pakistan or Sri Lanka. The IMF, having approved a $4.7bn program in January 2023, is Bangladesh's key multilateral partner shaping fiscal policy, a relationship that intensified after the August 2024 political transition as the interim Yunus government sought both continuity and additional support. India, long a close ally under the Hasina government and a haven for her since her August 2024 ouster, has seen relations cool markedly with the interim government, which has publicly criticized New Delhi's continued hosting of the former prime minister -- a tension that stands in contrast to Bangladesh's otherwise stable Western trade relationships.", 'sources': [('IMF: $4.7bn Bangladesh loan approval, January 2023', 'https://www.tbsnews.net/economy/imf-approves-bangladeshs-45-billion-loan-proposal-576942'), ("BSS News: Bangladesh's RMG exports FY25 figures", 'https://www.bssnews.net/at-a-glance/291544'), ("Textile Focus: 11 countries take nearly 80% of Bangladesh's RMG exports", 'https://textilefocus.com/billion-dollar-markets-11-countries-now-take-nearly-80-of-bangladeshs-rmg-exports/'), ('House of Commons Library: Bangladesh -- the fall of the Hasina government', 'https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10096/')]},
    'BHR': {'summary': "Bahrain is the only GCC state that functions as a net debtor rather than net creditor: its public debt reached an estimated 146.8% of GDP by end-2025, the second-highest of any Fitch-rated sovereign worldwide, financed through a mix of domestic and international bond issuance plus direct bilateral support from Gulf neighbors rather than an IMF program (the IMF conducts only Article IV surveillance). Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Kuwait are Bahrain's most important creditors and geopolitical patrons, having jointly extended a $10 billion, interest-free Fiscal Balance Program loan in October 2018 -- worth roughly a quarter of Bahrain's GDP -- conditioned on deficit-reduction reforms including VAT introduction and a 2022 VAT doubling to 10%. Saudi Arabia is also Bahrain's dominant trade partner and, since the March 2011 Peninsula Shield intervention that helped end the Bahraini uprising, its guarantor of internal political stability. The United States is Bahrain's principal Western security partner, hosting the US Navy's Fifth Fleet in Manama, and Bahrain was (with the UAE) among the first Gulf states to normalize relations with Israel under the September 2020 Abraham Accords.", 'sources': [('Forbes, Bahrain Tries To Reset Its Economy, With $10B Aid Package From Richer Neighbors', 'https://www.forbes.com/sites/dominicdudley/2018/10/05/bahrain-reset-economy-10b-aid-package/'), ("Al Arabiya, Fitch downgrades Bahrain to 'B' on rising debt, wider deficits", 'https://english.alarabiya.net/business/economy/2026/02/23/fitch-downgrades-bahrain-to-b-on-rising-debt-wider-deficits'), ("Investing.com, Bahrain's credit rating lowered to 'B' at S&P on high debt and fiscal deficits", 'https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/bahrains-credit-rating-lowered-to-b-at-sp-on-high-debt-and-fiscal-deficits-93CH-4373781'), ('Gulf News, Bahrain increases VAT from 5% to 10%', 'https://gulfnews.com/business/bahrain-increases-vat-from-5-to-10-1.1638968042498')]},
    'BTN': {'summary': "India is, without real parallel elsewhere in the region, Bhutan's dominant partner across every dimension of the economy: main trading partner, primary aid donor, sole market for hydropower exports, and overwhelmingly the largest creditor. India-financed hydropower loans -- chiefly for the delayed and cost-overrun Punatsangchhu-I and -II projects -- make up roughly 60-65% of Bhutan's external debt, which stood at about 100.5% of GDP as of June 2025 per the IMF's 2025 Article IV consultation (concluded January 2026). Bhutan has never entered a full IMF lending program, relying solely on periodic Article IV surveillance, a reflection of its unusually conservative, aid-and-grant-financed fiscal history. Unlike Nepal, Sri Lanka or the Maldives, Bhutan maintains no formal diplomatic relations with China and has almost no independent economic relationship with Beijing, making it the one country in the region essentially outside the India-China competitive dynamic -- though the newly announced Gelephu Mindfulness City project (December 2023) signals an explicit attempt to attract diversified international investment beyond India for the first time.", 'sources': [('IMF: Bhutan concludes 2024 Article IV consultation', 'https://imf.org/en/News/Articles/2024/09/19/pr-24336-bhutan-imf-concludes-2024-article-iv-consultation'), ('IMF: Bhutan concludes 2025 Article IV consultation', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/01/26/pr-2619-bhutan-imf-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation'), ("Newsreel Asia: Bhutan's rising debt crisis tied to India-funded hydropower projects", 'https://newsreel.asia/articles/bhutan-rising-debt-crisis-india-loans-hydropower'), ("Scroll.in: India's renewable energy ambitions has ended up wedding Bhutan to hydropower despite rising costs", 'https://scroll.in/article/1045362/indias-renewable-energy-ambitions-has-ended-up-wedding-bhutan-to-hydropower-despite-rising-costs'), ("TIME: Inside Bhutan's plan to boost its economy with 'mindful capitalism'", 'https://time.com/7204652/gelephu-mindfulness-city-bhutan-economy/')]},
    'DZA': {'summary': "Algeria carries very limited external debt by regional standards (public debt reached 52.1% of GDP only in 2026 amid widening deficits, per the IMF's Article IV mission) and has deliberately avoided Eurobond or IMF borrowing, instead running a 'no-borrowing' policy financed from hydrocarbon-export reserves (an estimated $71bn in 2024, though falling by over $12bn between September 2024 and July 2025). The largest foreign investors are Italy's Eni (gas, via Sonatrach partnership boosting Transmed exports toward 9 bcm/year) and China, which overtook France as Algeria's top import-source country in 2024 ($11.68bn in Chinese exports to Algeria) and is financing large infrastructure projects (the completed 1,216km East-West highway, a new $7bn Bled El Hadba phosphate megaproject). Because Algeria seeks no IMF or Gulf financing, its main external 'ally' relationships are commercial/energy-driven — Italy for gas exports, China for infrastructure and imports — rather than aid-based; Russia remains a longstanding defense-cooperation partner though not a major economic one. Algeria's central geopolitical rival is Morocco, over the Western Sahara dispute: the two severed diplomatic ties in August 2021, closed their shared land border since 1994, and Algeria has separately entered a serious diplomatic rupture with France (2024-2025) after Paris backed Morocco's autonomy plan and detained-then-pardoned writer Boualem Sansal, which has damaged a historically important trade and migration relationship with Algeria's former colonial power. The most consequential recent economic development is arguably the France rupture itself, which by 2025 had led Algeria to end special French property rights and reject older bilateral agreements, accelerating its pivot toward China and Gulf-adjacent partners.", 'sources': [('IMF — 2026 Article IV mission to Algeria', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/07/06/pr26236-algeria-imf-staff-completes-2026-article-iv-mission'), ('Energy Connects — Eni-Sonatrach gas export deal, 2022', 'https://www.energyconnects.com/news/gas-lng/2022/may/eni-and-sonatrach-sign-deal-to-boost-algeria-gas-exports-to-italy/'), ('Silk Road Briefing / SASAC — China-Algeria trade and BRI projects', 'http://en.sasac.gov.cn/2024/07/25/c_17553.htm'), ('Middle East Eye — France-Algeria diplomatic crisis', 'https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/france-algeria-most-serious-diplomatic-crisis-end-colonial-rule'), ('Al Jazeera — Algeria severs diplomatic ties with Morocco, 2021', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/8/24/algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-morocco')]},
    'EGY': {'summary': "Egypt's external debt (about $152.9bn as of June 2024) is held through a mix of Eurobonds (roughly $19.6bn in USD-denominated bonds plus about $3.9bn in euro-denominated bonds as of December 2024), multilateral lenders (IMF, World Bank), and — critically — long-term Gulf central-bank deposits, which stood at $9.3bn in mid-2024 (Saudi Arabia $5.3bn, Kuwait $4.0bn). The largest foreign direct investors are Gulf sovereign wealth funds: the UAE's ADQ (the $35bn Ras El-Hekma real-estate/coastal development deal of February 2024, converting roughly $11bn of existing deposits into equity) and Qatar's Qatari Diar (a roughly $29.7bn investment framework), both targeting real estate and tourism infrastructure. Debt rollover and budget support come primarily from the IMF's $8bn Extended Fund Facility (seventh review completed July 2026, unlocking $1.8bn and bringing cumulative disbursements to about $7.3bn) working in tandem with Gulf deposits, which together de-risked what the IMF itself has called a program contingent on continued Gulf goodwill. Egypt's principal geopolitical allies are the Gulf monarchies (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Qatar, Kuwait) — who value Cairo's regional weight and security cooperation — alongside the United States and, on trade, the EU (Italy, Germany); Egypt has no acute bilateral state rival on the scale of Algeria-Morocco, but its risk profile is highly exposed to regional conflict spillover, illustrated by the Houthi Red Sea campaign cutting Suez Canal revenue roughly in half by early 2026. The single most consequential recent deal remains the 2024 Ras El-Hekma transaction, which simultaneously resolved a domestic FX crisis and functioned as an implicit debt-for-equity swap with the UAE.", 'sources': [('Reuters via KSAT — Egypt floats currency, IMF expands loan to $8bn (March 2024)', 'https://www.ksat.com/business/2024/03/06/egypt-floats-its-currency-and-agrees-with-the-imf-to-increase-a-bailout-loan-to-8-billion/'), ('Daily News Egypt — CBE external debt developments, 2024', 'https://www.dailynewsegypt.com/2025/08/23/cbe-reports-developments-in-egypts-external-debt-in-2024/'), ('ADQ — $35bn Ras El-Hekma consortium investment', 'https://www.adq.ae/newsroom/adq-led-consortium-to-invest-usd-35-billion-in-egypt/'), ('IMF — Seventh EFF/second RSF review completed, July 2026', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/07/30/pr26271-egypt-imf-completes-the-7th-review-under-eff-and-2nd-review-under-the-rsf'), ('The National / Al Majalla — Suez Canal revenue collapse from Red Sea disruption', 'https://en.majalla.com/node/329123/business-economy/egypt%E2%80%99s-economy-2025-tests-and-2026-bets')]},
    'IND': {'summary': "India carries no IMF lending program and relies instead on periodic Article IV surveillance, reflecting its position as a net capital-market borrower rather than a Fund-dependent economy; the IMF nonetheless remains an influential voice on India's growth and trade outlook, projecting 6.6% growth for FY2026 before the February 2026 US-India tariff deal improved the picture. The United States is India's most important single economic partner -- its largest export market for both IT services and pharmaceuticals, and the source of a major 2025 tariff dispute (tariffs reached 25% before being cut to about 18% under the February 2026 deal). Mauritius and Singapore are the largest sources of foreign direct investment, together with the US accounting for the bulk of India's $81.04bn in FY2024-25 FDI inflows, though this partly reflects tax-treaty routing rather than underlying economic ties. China remains India's most significant strategic rival -- relations have not fully recovered from the deadly June 2020 Galwan Valley border clash, which triggered Indian bans on Chinese apps and tighter investment screening -- while Pakistan is India's most acute security rival, with the April-May 2025 Pahalgam attack and Operation Sindoor conflict marking the most serious bilateral escalation in years.", 'sources': [("CNBC: India's economy grows 8.2% in September quarter even as tariffs bite", 'https://www.cnbc.com/2025/11/28/india-economy-gdp-grows-september-quarter-tariffs-bite-trade-us-modi.html'), ("Goldman Sachs: Outlook for India's economy in 2026 amid new US trade deal", 'https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/articles/the-outlook-for-indias-economy-in-2026-amid-new-us-tradedeal'), ('PIB: India records $81.04bn FDI inflow in FY2024-25', 'https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2131716&reg=48&lang=2'), ("Business Standard: Singapore/Mauritius as India's top FDI sources", 'https://www.business-standard.com/amp/economy/news/india-crosses-1-trillion-in-fdi-since-2000-mauritius-tops-the-list-124121300684_1.html'), ('Carnegie Endowment: The Road from Galwan', 'https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2021/03/the-road-from-galwan-the-future-of-india-china-relations'), ('Stimson Center: Four Days in May -- the India-Pakistan crisis of 2025', 'https://www.stimson.org/2025/four-days-in-may-the-india-pakistan-crisis-of-2025/')]},
    'IRN': {'summary': "Iran has had no IMF financing arrangement, and has not completed a full Article IV consultation since March 2018, reflecting both US/UN sanctions and Tehran's own reluctance to expose macroeconomic and reserve data to IMF scrutiny; occasional informal IMF Board briefings (such as one in December 2022) have substituted for the mandatory Article IV process for years. China is Iran's most important economic partner by a wide margin, buying an estimated 90% of Iranian crude oil via direct purchases and 'teapot' refineries in Shandong province, and serving as Iran's largest source of imports and, under a 2021, 25-year cooperation framework, infrastructure investment. Russia is Iran's closest strategic partner, cooperating militarily and diplomatically, including jointly resisting the E3's August 2025 UN snapback sanctions at the Security Council alongside China. Iran's central adversarial economic relationship is with the United States, whose 2018 JCPOA withdrawal and 'maximum pressure' sanctions campaign, followed by the September 2025 UN snapback and the February 2026 US-Israeli war, drove Iran's crude exports to zero and its currency to record lows (near 1.9 million rial per dollar by April 2026); Israel and the E3 (UK, France, Germany) are Iran's other principal adversaries. The UAE and Oman occupy a more neutral, transactional position -- both host significant re-export and financial-intermediary trade with Iran despite the sanctions regime, and Oman brokered both the April 2026 US-Iran ceasefire and an August 2026 temporary Strait of Hormuz transit and mine-clearing arrangement. As of August 2026, no clear resolution -- diplomatic or military -- to the conflict or the broader sanctions standoff was in sight, the ceasefire having collapsed and been only partially restored since April.", 'sources': [('IMF (2018 Article IV Consultation, last completed)', 'https://www.imf.org/en/News/Articles/2018/03/29/pr18114-iran-imf-executive-board-concludes-2018-article-iv-consultation'), ('Council of the EU (Iran sanctions snapback, Sept 2025)', 'https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2025/09/29/iran-sanctions-snapback-council-reimposes-restrictive-measures/'), ('United Against Nuclear Iran (Chinese purchasers of Iranian oil)', 'https://www.unitedagainstnucleariran.com/blog/uncovering-chinese-purchasers-of-iranian-oil'), ("CNBC (Iran's economy in charts, 2026 war)", 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/23/iran-economy-war-charts-rial-oil-strait-hormuz-blockade.html'), ('CNN (Iran-Oman Hormuz talks, Aug 2026)', 'https://www.cnn.com/2026/08/04/world/live-news/iran-war-trump'), ('Congress.gov CRS (U.S. Sanctions on Iran)', 'https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12452')]},
    'IRQ': {'summary': "Iraq holds no active IMF financing arrangement; its last was a $5.34bn Stand-By Arrangement approved in July 2016 that expired in 2019, and Baghdad has since relied only on annual IMF Article IV surveillance (most recently concluded July 9, 2025) rather than seeking new lending, reflecting relatively comfortable, if oil-price-dependent, reserves. Roughly 80% of Iraq's Saddam-era Paris Club debt was written off in a landmark 2004 agreement, while Gulf War reparations to Kuwait -- administered by the UN Compensation Commission, which had awarded $46bn in claims -- have been largely settled. China and India are Iraq's dominant trade and investment partners as its two largest crude buyers, while the United States retains outsized leverage over Iraq's financial system because Iraqi dollar reserves and oil-sale proceeds clear through a Federal Reserve Bank of New York account, a chokepoint Washington has used to sanction banks accused of funneling dollars to Iran (2022-2024). Iraq's most fraught economic relationship is with Iran, source of essential electricity and gas imports but also the target of a series of U.S. Treasury bank sanctions, while Baghdad also has an unresolved revenue-sharing dispute with its own Kurdistan Regional Government that halted the KRG's oil exports via Turkey from March 2023 into 2026. The February 2026 outbreak of the US-Israeli war on Iran, which drew in Iran-aligned Iraqi militias and collapsed national oil output to under 900,000 bpd by June 2026, has left Iraq newly reliant on emergency fiscal management and estimates of roughly $200bn in investment needed over five years to restore 7 million bpd of capacity.", 'sources': [('IMF Press Release 25/243 (2025 Article IV)', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/07/08/pr-25243-iraq-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation'), ('Reuters via Business Standard (2016 SBA)', 'https://www.business-standard.com/article/reuters/imf-board-approves-5-34-billion-stand-by-arrangement-for-iraq-116070800049_1.html'), ('Atlantic Council (Kurdistan budget/oil dispute)', 'https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/in-depth-research-reports/report/kurdistan-iraq-2024-budget-not-what-it-appears-when-it-first-meets-the-eye/'), ('Al Jazeera (dinar/US Treasury bank bans)', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/7/27/protesters-banks-demand-action-after-iraqi-dinar-plunges-post-us-sanctions'), ('AGBI (2026 war economic impact)', 'https://www.agbi.com/opinion/economy/2026/07/as-the-war-flares-iraqs-economic-dire-straits-deepen/'), ('Council on Foreign Relations (Iraq debt/Paris Club background)', 'https://www.cfr.org/backgrounders/iraq-regimes-debt')]},
    'ISR': {'summary': "Israel is a developed, investment-grade economy with no IMF borrowing arrangement -- the Fund's role is limited to regular Article IV surveillance, most recently concluded in mid-2026 -- but it does carry substantial sovereign debt, which rose from 60% of GDP at end-2022 to about 68% at end-2025 as war costs mounted, financed through domestic and international bond markets rather than multilateral lending. The United States is Israel's most important economic and security partner, providing $3.8 billion annually in military assistance under the 2019-2028 Memorandum of Understanding and remaining the top destination for Israeli high-tech exports and the primary broker (with Qatar and Egypt) of the 2023-2025 Gaza ceasefire agreements. Foreign -- overwhelmingly American and European -- venture capital funds roughly 90% of Israeli high-tech investment, making the sector (17% of GDP, over half of exports) acutely sensitive to perceived political and security risk. All three major credit-rating agencies (Moody's, S&P, Fitch) downgraded Israel for the first time in its history during 2024 as war costs -- estimated at $66 billion, or over 12% of GDP, through 2025 -- mounted from the Gaza war, the Hezbollah conflict, and direct confrontation with Iran, which is Israel's principal adversary and escalated to a direct 12-day war in June 2025.", 'sources': [("CNN Business, Israel's economy slumps 19.4% in Q4 amid Gaza war", 'https://www.cnn.com/2024/02/19/economy/israel-gdp-war-economy/index.html'), ("Bloomberg, Moody's downgrades Israel again as economic costs of war mount", 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-09-27/israel-is-downgraded-by-moody-s-again-as-war-takes-economic-toll'), ('CFR, U.S. Aid to Israel in Four Charts', 'https://www.cfr.org/articles/us-aid-israel-four-charts'), ('Israel Innovation Authority, The State of High-Tech 2025', 'https://innovationisrael.org.il/en/report/the-state-of-high-tech-2025/'), ('IMF, Israel: IMF Executive Board Concludes 2026 Article IV Consultation', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/07/01/pr26234-israel-imf-executive-board-concludes-2026-article-iv-consultation')]},
    'JOR': {'summary': "Jordan's public debt (about 90.2% of GDP in 2024, projected to reach 92% by 2026 before an IMF-targeted decline to 80% by 2028) is held through a mix of Eurobonds — including a $700m issuance in November 2025 at a record-low 5.75% coupon — concessional multilateral loans, and bilateral deposits, with the government actively retiring costlier Eurobonds early using cheaper concessional financing to save an estimated $40m/year. The United States is Jordan's most important economic and strategic partner, providing about $845m/year in budget and security support under a 2022 seven-year MOU (the largest per-capita U.S. assistance outside Israel), while Gulf states (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Kuwait) are both major investors/aid providers (e.g., a $2.5bn joint pledge in 2018) and the top destination for Jordanian potash exports and expatriate remittances. Debt rollover and reform support come from the IMF's current $1.2bn Extended Fund Facility (approved January 2024, fifth review completed June 2026, on track throughout) alongside a further $1.1bn in new World Bank financing announced in 2025 for economic-reform and social-protection projects. Jordan's principal allies are the U.S. and the Gulf monarchies, reflecting its role as a security anchor amid regional turmoil, and it has no direct bilateral-rivalry equivalent to Algeria-Morocco; instead its acute risk exposure is to conflict spillover it does not control — the Gaza war (2023-2024, which cost the tourism sector an estimated 70,000 jobs) and the 2025-2026 Israel-Iran war, both of which have repeatedly hit tourism revenue and disrupted Red Sea shipping through the port of Aqaba. The clearest recent evidence of continued market confidence despite these shocks is Jordan's oversubscribed, lowest-ever-spread November 2025 Eurobond sale.", 'sources': [('IMF — Jordan EFF approved, January 2024', 'https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2024/06/28/jordan-first-review-under-the-extended-arrangement-under-the-extended-fund-facility-and-551212'), ('IMF — Fifth EFF/second RSF review completed, June 2026', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/06/17/pr26211-jordan-imf-completes-5th-review-under-eff-and-2nd-review-under-rsf-arrangements'), ('Zawya — Jordan completes Eurobond repayment under debt strategy', 'https://www.zawya.com/en/economy/levant/jordan-govt-completes-eurobond-repayment-under-debt-management-strategy-roy4a1t6'), ('Washington Institute — The Jordan exception in U.S. foreign assistance', 'https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/jordan-exception-us-foreign-assistance'), ("The National — Iran war casts shadow over Jordan's Aqaba economic hopes, July 2026", 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/mena/2026/07/24/aqaba-jordan-us-iran-war/')]},
    'KWT': {'summary': "Kuwait is a net external creditor with no IMF borrowing; the Kuwait Investment Authority (KIA), which manages the General Reserve Fund and the Future Generations Fund (established 1976), surpassed $1 trillion in assets under management in mid-2025 (up from $846 billion a year earlier), making it the world's fifth-largest sovereign wealth fund. After nearly a decade of parliamentary refusal to pass debt-enabling legislation forced Kuwait to draw down the General Reserve Fund from roughly $150 billion toward depletion by 2020-21, the suspension of the National Assembly in 2024 allowed the government to pass a new Public Debt Law in March 2025 (raising the borrowing ceiling to about $98 billion) and return to global bond markets with an $11.25 billion issuance -- its first international bond sale since 2017. China, South Korea, Japan, and India are Kuwait's dominant oil-export markets and thus its most consequential trade partners, while the United States remains Kuwait's principal security guarantor since the 1991 Gulf War liberation. Saudi Arabia and the UAE are Kuwait's closest GCC allies, cooperating on OPEC+ quotas and jointly managing the Neutral Zone oil fields with Saudi Arabia specifically.", 'sources': [("Bloomberg, Kuwait gets first Moody's downgrade over 'liquidity risks'", 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2020-09-23/kuwait-gets-first-downgrade-from-moody-s-over-liquidity-risks'), ('Arab News, Kuwait raises $11.25bn in first global bond sale since 2017', 'https://www.arabnews.com/node/2617534/amp'), ('Arab News, Kuwait passes borrowing law to rejoin global debt markets after 8 years', 'https://www.arabnews.com/node/2595019/business-economy'), ('IMF, Kuwait: 2025 Article IV Consultation', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/02/23/pr-26061-kuwait-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation'), ('Times Kuwait, KIA surpasses $1 trillion mark amid bold global investment strategy', 'https://timeskuwait.com/kia-surpasses-1-trillion-mark-amid-bold-global-investment-strategy/')]},
    'LBN': {'summary': "Lebanon defaulted on all of its roughly $31bn in Eurobonds in March 2020, and as of late 2025 an estimated 40% of at least one defaulted note was foreign-held, per Oxford Economics, alongside domestic bank holders squeezed by an insolvent Banque du Liban; a genuine restructuring requires resolving a standoff between the central bank (which wants depositors, not Eurobond holders, prioritized) and foreign creditors who could otherwise pursue New York litigation. Lebanon has no active IMF program: a 2022 staff-level agreement for a $3bn Extended Fund Facility was never brought to the IMF Board because required reforms (bank resolution law, unified exchange rate, capital-controls legislation) were not fully implemented, and the IMF said in September 2025 that Lebanon's newly passed bank-resolution law still needed refinement. Gulf states, chiefly Saudi Arabia and Qatar, are Lebanon's biggest prospective investors and creditors of last resort, having pledged in 2025 to fund a south Lebanon economic development zone once Hezbollah disarms -- an explicit linkage of investment to a security outcome. The United States is central to both Lebanon's security diplomacy (brokering the November 2024 Israel-Hezbollah ceasefire) and its financial isolation of Hezbollah's networks, while Iran, via its Hezbollah patronage, is Lebanon's most consequential adversarial relationship given the group's role in the October 2023-November 2024 war (an estimated $14bn in damage and losses) and the risk of renewed conflict amid the 2026 Iran war. Lebanon's most important 2025-2026 development is the parallel, unresolved process of banking-sector reform and Hezbollah disarmament, both explicit preconditions Western and Gulf actors have set for any large-scale financial rescue.", 'sources': [('IMF Press Release 22/108 (2022 staff-level agreement)', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/04/07/pr22108-imf-reaches-agreement-on-economic-policies-with-lebanon-for-a-four-year-fund-facility'), ('The National (IMF presses Lebanon on banking reforms, Sept 2025)', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2025/09/26/imf-presses-lebanon-to-improve-banking-reforms/'), ('Washington Times (Saudi Arabia, Qatar investment pledge)', 'https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2025/aug/26/saudi-arabia-qatar-invest-lebanon-economic-zone-disarmed-hezbollah-us/'), ('World Bank / Times of Israel (reconstruction cost, war damage)', 'https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/world-bank-reconstruction-of-lebanon-after-israel-hezbollah-war-to-cost-11-billion/'), ('Council on Foreign Relations (Lebanon backgrounder)', 'https://www.cfr.org/backgrounders/lebanon-how-israel-hezbollah-and-regional-powers-are-shaping-its-future'), ('CIDOB (Lebanon financial crisis / national collapse)', 'https://www.cidob.org/en/publications/lebanon-financial-crisis-or-national-collapse')]},
    'LBY': {'summary': "Libya carries relatively low conventional sovereign debt by regional standards, but its sovereign wealth fund, the Libyan Investment Authority (LIA, roughly $62.85bn in assets), has been frozen under UN Security Council sanctions since the 2011 revolution; as of the mid-2020s, only about half the LIA's assets -- some $20-23bn unrestricted plus $9.5bn under specific licenses -- are actively usable, with the Security Council only beginning to approve limited reform requests from 2025 onward. Libya has no IMF financing arrangement and does not appear to be actively seeking one; the IMF's 2025 and 2026 Article IV consultations (concluded June 25, 2025 and June 22, 2026 respectively) were conducted on a lapse-of-time basis and explicitly cited continued political division as rendering ambitious reform 'next to impossible.' Italy is Libya's most important economic partner and investor, both as top buyer of its crude and home to Eni, its leading foreign oil operator since 1959, while France (TotalEnergies) and the US (ConocoPhillips) are also major upstream investors under a new 25-year Waha agreement signed in January 2026. Libya's foreign relationships mirror its internal political split: Turkey backs the Tripoli-based Government of National Unity militarily and economically, while the UAE, Egypt and Russia have backed the eastern-based administration and Libyan National Army, with Russia's state printer Goznak having printed billions of dinars for the eastern central bank between 2015 and 2020 outside Tripoli's control. A key 2025-2026 development is the partial reunification of institutions (the central bank formally reunified in August 2023, though a second governor crisis erupted in August-October 2024) alongside surging foreign energy investment even as UN-backed efforts to hold national elections remain stalled by the same Tripoli-east rivalry.", 'sources': [('International Crisis Group (Frozen Billions: LIA sanctions)', 'https://www.crisisgroup.org/middle-east-north-africa/north-africa/libya/249-frozen-billions-reforming-sanctions-libyan-investment-authority'), ('IMF (2025 Article IV Consultation-Press Release, Libya)', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/06/25/pr-25217-libya-imf-executive-board-concludes-the-2025-article-iv-consultation'), ('IMF (2026 Article IV Concluding Statement, Libya)', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/04/10/mcs-04102026-libya-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2026-article-iv-consultation-mission'), ("SeaNews (Libya Italy's top crude supplier 2025)", 'https://www.seanews.com.tr/article/libya-becomes-italys-big-crude-oil-supplier-mmkx16hk'), ("Middle East Eye (Libya's rival central bank branches reunite)", 'https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/libyas-rival-central-bank-branches-reunite'), ('The National (Libya central bank feud, Aug 2024)', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/mena/2024/08/30/how-libyas-unfolding-central-bank-feud-could-escalate-tension/')]},
    'LKA': {'summary': "China and Japan are Sri Lanka's largest official bilateral creditors, both central to the multi-year debt restructuring completed in stages through 2023-2024; China's 99-year Hambantota port lease (finalized December 2017, $1.12bn) remains the emblematic example of Beijing's leverage over Sri Lankan infrastructure, even though the transaction was a lease rather than a debt-for-equity default swap. India positioned itself as a first-responder financier during the acute 2022 crisis with emergency credit lines and has since co-chaired the Official Creditor Committee (alongside Japan and France) that reached restructuring agreements in principle in November 2023, directly competing with China for long-term economic and port influence. The IMF is the central multilateral anchor via its $3bn, 48-month Extended Fund Facility approved in March 2023, with a fifth-and-sixth-review staff-level agreement reached in April 2026 indicating the program remains on track. The United States and European Union are Sri Lanka's largest export markets for apparel, while the Gulf states (Iraq, UAE) and Russia/Turkey are the dominant destinations for its tea exports -- a diversification that has partly insulated Sri Lanka's tea trade from South Asian geopolitics even as its debt and infrastructure sit squarely within the India-China rivalry.", 'sources': [('CSIS: Game of Loans -- how China bought Hambantota', 'https://www.csis.org/analysis/game-loans-how-china-bought-hambantota'), ("IMF: Sri Lanka's Sovereign Debt Restructuring -- Lessons from Complex Processes", 'https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/wp/2025/english/wpiea2025175-source-pdf.pdf'), ('IMF: Sri Lanka EFF approval, March 2023', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2023/03/20/pr2379-imf-executive-board-approves-under-the-new-eff-arrangement-for-sri-lanka'), ('IMF: Sri Lanka completes third review under EFF, February 2025', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/02/28/pr25053-sri-lanka-imf-completes-the-3rd-rev-under-the-eff'), ('IMF: Sri Lanka staff-level agreement on combined fifth/sixth reviews, April 2026', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/04/09/pr26113-sri-lanka-imf-staff-reaches-sla-combined-fifth-and-sixth-reviews-under-eff-arrangement')]},
    'MAR': {'summary': "Morocco's public debt (about 70% of GDP in 2024, trending toward 68% by 2026) is held through a mix of domestic debt and international Eurobonds, including a €2bn issuance in 2025 — Morocco's first international bond sale since 2020 — earmarked for reform and World Cup 2030 infrastructure financing; among bilateral lenders, France is the largest, followed by Germany, Japan, and Spain. The EU is by far Morocco's biggest trade and investment partner (33.7% of 2025 goods trade), with Spain the single largest bilateral partner (€22.7bn trade in 2025) and France historically Morocco's top FDI source in autos and aerospace, though China has become a fast-growing manufacturing investor (the CDG-Gotion EV battery gigafactory, initial ~$1.3bn). Debt rollover and precautionary support come from the IMF's Flexible Credit Line — Morocco is the only MENA country eligible for this elite, no-conditionality facility, holding a $5bn FCL from 2023 and a reduced $4.5bn successor from April 2025, deliberately scaled down as part of Morocco's 'exit strategy' from Fund reliance. Morocco's chief geopolitical allies are the U.S. (which recognized Moroccan sovereignty over Western Sahara in December 2020), France (which reversed decades of neutrality to back Morocco's autonomy plan in July 2024, followed by an October 2024 Macron state visit), and Spain (which endorsed the same autonomy plan in March 2022) — a diplomatic sweep that has progressively isolated Morocco's principal rival, Algeria, over the Western Sahara dispute. The most consequential recent economic story, however, is domestic: the September-October 2025 'Gen Z 212' protests, in which public anger over World Cup stadium spending versus hospital underfunding forced the government to announce a $15bn increase in health and education spending.", 'sources': [('IMF — New two-year $4.5bn FCL for Morocco, April 2025', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/04/02/pr25085-morocco-imf-executive-board-approves-new-two-year-flexible-credit-line-arrangement'), ("Bloomberg — Morocco's first Eurobond sale since 2020", 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-02-20/morocco-plans-euro-debt-in-first-foreign-bond-sale-in-two-years'), ('Maroc.ma — France-Morocco Western Sahara turning point, 2024', 'https://www.maroc.ma/en/news/france-moroccan-sahara-major-strategic-turning-point'), ('Yabiladi — Morocco-Spain trade reaches €22.7bn in 2025', 'https://en.yabiladi.com/articles/details/201251/morocco-spain-trade-reaches-billion-2025.html'), ('CNN — Gen Z 212 protests over World Cup spending, October 2025', 'https://www.cnn.com/2025/10/01/world/morocco-youth-protest-world-cup-spending-latam-intl')]},
    'MDV': {'summary': "China and India are simultaneously Maldives' largest creditors and its two most actively competing geopolitical patrons, together holding the bulk of the country's roughly $3.4bn in external debt (China about $1.3bn, India about $130 million) within a total public-debt stock that reached $9.4bn (over 134% of GDP) by Q4 2024. Rather than pursue a full IMF program -- the Fund has engaged only through Article IV surveillance, most recently concluding a mission in June 2026 -- Male has instead extracted bilateral relief from both major creditors: India extended a $554 million line of credit and cut Maldives' annual debt-service burden to India by 40% in 2025, while China finalized a five-year deferral of Chinese debts in October 2025. President Muizzu's 2023 election on an explicitly pro-China, anti-India 'India Out' platform marked the high point of the pivot toward Beijing, but the costly 2024 #BoycottMaldives tourism fallout (a roughly 42% drop in Indian arrivals, costing up to $2bn) and the deepening debt crisis forced a partial and pragmatic re-engagement with New Delhi through 2025-2026. Tourism-source-market composition is itself a geopolitical variable given India's historical position as the single largest visitor market, making the India-China contest over the Maldives arguably the most economically immediate of any bilateral rivalry covered in this dataset.", 'sources': [("Tribune India / ANI: China's lending practices push Maldives toward sovereign default", 'https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/china/chinas-lending-practices-push-maldives-toward-sovereign-default'), ('East Asia Forum: Maldives-India political fallout tests tourism ties', 'https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/05/09/maldives-india-political-fallout-tests-tourism-ties/'), ('Al Jazeera: Mohamed Muizzu wins Maldives election in victory for pro-China camp', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/9/30/maldives-opposition-candidate-muiz-wins-presidential-runoff'), ('Tribune India: India extends financial lifeline to Maldives', 'https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/india/india-extends-financial-lifeline-to-maldives-with-rs-832-crore-aid'), ('IMF: Maldives 2026 Article IV mission concluded', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/06/16/pr-26208-maldives-imf-staff-completes-2026-article-iv-mission')]},
    'NPL': {'summary': "India is by far Nepal's dominant economic partner: the destination for essentially all hydropower exports (under a January 2024 ten-year, 10,000 MW power-trade agreement), the source of the large majority of imports, and Nepal's only practical transit route to the sea given its landlocked geography. This dependence has periodically been weaponized, most severely during the unofficial 2015-2016 Indian border blockade that followed Nepal's post-earthquake constitutional dispute, which shut some 2,200 industries and cost an estimated $5bn. China serves as Nepal's main geopolitical counterweight, courted for infrastructure financing and diplomatic balance, though its practical economic footprint remains far smaller than India's. The IMF, via a $395.9 million Extended Credit Facility approved in January 2022 and extended through further reviews (a sixth review completed October 2025, with an ECF extension approved December 2025), has provided continuous macro-fiscal support through the 2025 Gen-Z protests and political transition. Remittances -- reaching an all-time monthly high above Rs 200 billion in November 2025 -- are arguably Nepal's single most important external economic relationship in aggregate, spread across Gulf states, Malaysia, and India as host-country destinations for millions of Nepali migrant workers.", 'sources': [('Kathmandu Post: Nepal signs deal with India to export 10,000 MW power in 10 years', 'https://kathmandupost.com/national/2024/01/04/nepal-india-ink-10-year-10-000mw-power-trade'), ('Conciliation Resources: Uncertain aftermath -- political impacts of the 2015 earthquakes in Nepal', 'https://www.c-r.org/accord/nepal/uncertain-aftermath-political-impacts-2015-earthquakes-nepal'), ('IMF: Nepal ECF approval, January 2022', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/01/13/pr2206-nepal-imf-executive-board-approves-us-million-ecf-arrangement'), ('IMF: Nepal completes sixth ECF review, October 2025', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/10/02/pr-25327-nepal-imf-executive-board-completes-the-sixth-review-under-the-ecf-arrangement'), ('Kathmandu Post: Remittances to Nepal top Rs 200 billion a month for first time', 'https://kathmandupost.com/money/2025/11/16/nepal-s-monthly-remittances-top-rs200-billion-for-first-time')]},
    'OMN': {'summary': "Oman does not have an active IMF lending arrangement -- the Fund's role is limited to regular Article IV surveillance, most recently concluded in January 2026 -- but unlike Gulf net creditors, Oman was until recently a genuine sovereign borrower, having built public debt to roughly 68% of GDP by 2020 through a mix of domestic and international bond and sukuk issuance and bilateral Gulf support before an aggressive fiscal consolidation cut debt to about 34% of GDP by end-2024. The UAE is Oman's most important trade and investment partner, its top source of imports and top destination for non-oil exports and re-exports. China is Oman's most significant strategic investor through the Belt-and-Road-linked Duqm Special Economic Zone, where the Oman Wanfang consortium committed roughly $10.7 billion in 2016 to build a port, industrial park, and residential city, though development has proceeded more slowly than initially announced. Investment-grade rating restorations by S&P (September 2024), Fitch, and Moody's (July 2025) mark the most consequential recent validation of Oman's economic turnaround and materially lowered its cost of capital in international markets.", 'sources': [("Oman Observer, Oman's Medium-Term Fiscal Plan 2020-2024 explained", 'https://www.omanobserver.om/article/8495/Front%20Stories/omans-medium-term-fiscal-plan-2020-2024-explained'), ('MEED, Oman recovers investment-grade credit rating', 'https://www.meed.com/oman-recovers-investment-grade-credit-rating'), ("Oman Observer, Moody's upgrades Oman's credit rating to investment grade", 'https://www.omanobserver.om/article/1173296/business/economy/moodys-upgrades-omans-credit-rating-to-investment-grade'), ('IMF, Oman: 2025 Article IV Consultation concluding statement', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/01/14/pr-2604-oman-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consult'), ('HKTDC Belt and Road Portal, BRI-backed Oman economic zone attracts raft of global investors', 'https://beltandroad.hktdc.com/en/insights/bri-backed-oman-economic-zone-attracts-raft-global-investors')]},
    'PAK': {'summary': "China is Pakistan's largest single bilateral creditor, holding nearly $29bn in loans (World Bank data) mostly tied to CPEC energy and infrastructure projects, and has repeatedly rolled over maturing obligations -- including a $2bn rollover extended by one year in March 2025 and a further roughly $3.4bn rollover/refinancing in June 2025. Saudi Arabia and the UAE are the other critical rollover partners, holding multi-billion-dollar central-bank deposits that Islamabad depends on to bridge external financing gaps between IMF disbursements; the UAE agreed to roll over a $2bn deposit in January 2025 and UAE banks led a further $1bn fundraising in June 2025. The IMF itself is Pakistan's most important multilateral partner, currently anchoring the economy through the $7bn, 37-month Extended Fund Facility approved in September 2024. The United States remains Pakistan's largest single export market, chiefly for textiles, while China dominates the import side and remains Pakistan's principal strategic and military ally. India is Pakistan's clearest rival, with tension spiking into the four-day Operation Sindoor conflict in May 2025 following the Pahalgam attack; a newer, economically significant rivalry emerged in 2025-2026 with the Afghan Taliban government over a border war that cut bilateral trade from $2.46bn (2024) to $1.77bn (2025).", 'sources': [('World Bank International Debt Report (via analysis)', 'https://www.globalorder.live/post/understanding-pakistan-s-reliance-on-chinese-debt'), ('Dawn: China rolls over $2bn loan to Pakistan', 'https://www.dawn.com/news/1896661'), ('The News: Pakistan secures $3.4bn debt rollover from China', 'https://www.thenews.com.pk/latest/1325452-pakistan-secures-34bn-debt-rollover-from-china'), ('AGBI: Pakistan to repay matured loan deposits to UAE', 'https://www.agbi.com/finance/2026/04/pakistan-to-repay-matured-loan-deposits-to-uae/'), ('IMF: Pakistan EFF approval, September 2024', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2024/09/27/pr-24343-pakistan-imf-concludes-2024-aiv-consultation-pakistan-approves-37-mo-extended-arr'), ('Stimson Center: Four Days in May', 'https://www.stimson.org/2025/four-days-in-may-the-india-pakistan-crisis-of-2025/'), ("IANS Live: Afghanistan's new trade routes leave Pakistan's border economy reeling", 'https://ianslive.in/afghanistans-new-trade-routes-leave-pakistans-border-economy-reeling--20260825144348')]},
    'QAT': {'summary': "Qatar carries no IMF debt and is a substantial net external creditor; its main financial relationships are as an investor and equity partner rather than a borrower, with the Qatar Investment Authority (QIA) holding an estimated $510-560 billion in assets as of 2025, ranking among the world's ten largest sovereign wealth funds. QatarEnergy's international partners -- TotalEnergies (France), Shell (UK/Netherlands), ExxonMobil and ConocoPhillips (US), Eni (Italy), and Sinopec/CNPC (China) -- are Qatar's most important foreign investors, holding minority equity in North Field LNG expansion trains in exchange for long-term offtake rights. The United States is Qatar's principal security guarantor, hosting the region's largest US airbase at Al Udeid, even as the September 2025 Israeli strike on Hamas leaders in Doha exposed the limits of that security umbrella and strained the bilateral relationship at the margins. Qatar's biggest geopolitical distinctiveness among Gulf states is its continued engagement with Iran (with which it shares the North Field/South Pars gas reservoir), the Taliban, and Hamas's political leadership, a mediator role that generates outsized diplomatic capital but also friction with Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Bahrain, and Egypt, whose 2017-2021 blockade of Qatar was only resolved via the January 2021 Al-Ula Declaration.", 'sources': [('Brookings, The high cost of high stakes: Economic implications of the 2017 Gulf crisis', 'https://www.brookings.edu/articles/the-high-cost-of-high-stakes-economic-implications-of-the-2017-gulf-crisis/'), ('IFSWF, Qatar Investment Authority member profile', 'https://www.ifswf.org/member-profiles/qatar-investment-authority'), ('Offshore Technology, North Field Expansion Project, Qatar', 'https://www.offshore-technology.com/projects/north-field-expansion-project/'), ('CNN, Israel strikes Hamas leadership in Qatar in unprecedented attack', 'https://www.cnn.com/2025/09/10/middleeast/israel-strikes-hamas-qatar-explainer-intl-hnk')]},
    'SAU': {'summary': "Saudi Arabia holds no IMF or World Bank debt and is a net external creditor, financing itself instead through domestic and international sovereign bond and sukuk markets -- it raised $20 billion in international bonds in 2025 and plans record net issuance of about $25 billion in 2026 as it borrows to sustain Vision 2030 spending amid softer oil prices; public debt reached roughly SAR 1.52 trillion ($405 billion) by end-2025. The Public Investment Fund (PIF), with roughly $1.21 trillion in assets at end-2025, is both the kingdom's largest domestic investor and an increasingly important international one, targeting $2 trillion in AUM by 2030 and recently securing a $7 billion Islamic syndicated loan from 20 international banks. China is Saudi Arabia's most important trade and investment partner, the top destination for exports and top source of imports, and an increasingly prominent diplomatic actor, having brokered the March 2023 Saudi-Iran rapprochement. The United States remains the kingdom's primary security guarantor and a major recipient of PIF portfolio capital, while UK, French, and other Western defense and technology firms partner heavily on Vision 2030 projects. Iran is Saudi Arabia's principal regional rival, though the 2023 China-brokered detente has lowered -- without eliminating -- the risk of direct confrontation via proxies in Yemen and Lebanon. Egypt and Pakistan are recipients of substantial Saudi bilateral aid and deposits used to shore up their own balance-of-payments positions, reinforcing Riyadh's role as a regional lender even as it itself turns to global bond markets.", 'sources': [('IMF, Saudi Arabia: Concluding Statement of the 2025 Article IV Mission', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/06/25/saudi-arabia-concluding-statement-of-the-2025-article-iv-mission'), ("AGBI, Economists unfazed as Saudi Arabia's debt rises again", 'https://www.agbi.com/analysis/economy/2026/01/economists-unfazed-as-saudi-arabias-debt-rises-again/'), ('Bloomberg, Saudi Arabia plans $37bn borrowing spree to pay for huge projects', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-01-06/saudi-arabia-plans-37-billion-borrowing-spree-to-pay-for-huge-projects'), ('Arab News, Saudi non-oil exports to China surge 70% as trade ties deepen', 'https://www.arabnews.com/node/2591932/business-economy'), ('International Crisis Group, How Beijing Helped Riyadh and Tehran Reach a Detente', 'https://www.crisisgroup.org/qna/middle-east-north-africa-saudi-arabia-iran-china/how-beijing-helped-riyadh-and-tehran-reach-detente')]},
    'SYR': {'summary': "Syria has no active IMF or World Bank financing program: it has never issued Eurobonds, but carries an estimated $20-30bn (some estimates up to $50bn) in bilateral arrears, overwhelmingly to Iran (variously put at $16-50bn, the bulk of the total) and roughly $1.2bn to Russia -- debt the new government is auditing for legality rather than committing to repay on the old regime's terms. The World Bank cleared Syria's small IDA arrears in May 2025 (paid by Saudi Arabia and Qatar), restoring lending eligibility, and the IMF sent its first mission to Damascus since 2009 in November 2025, but no financing arrangement had been agreed as of mid-2026. Gulf states -- Qatar, Saudi Arabia and the UAE -- are now Syria's biggest foreign patrons, funding public-sector salaries, fuel and electricity purchases and reconstruction pledges, while Turkey is both a major trade partner and security guarantor along the northern border. The United States and EU, having lifted almost all sanctions in 2025 (the Caesar Act formally repealed December 18, 2025), are Syria's newest potential partners for investment and normalization, a sharp reversal after a decade as the country's principal sanctioning powers. Iran and Russia, the toppled Assad government's main lenders and military backers, have been largely shut out of the new order, making them Syria's most conspicuous economic and geopolitical estrangement. Syria's central 2025-2026 development is the World Bank's October 2025 estimate that reconstruction will cost $140-345bn (best estimate $216bn), a financing gap far exceeding any commitments made so far by Gulf, Turkish or Western donors.", 'sources': [('World Bank (reconstruction cost estimate, Oct 2025)', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/10/21/syria-s-post-conflict-reconstruction-costs-estimated-at-216-billion'), ("Amwaj Media (Syria's foreign debt to Iran)", 'https://amwaj.media/en/article/deep-dive-syria-s-foreign-debt-to-iran'), ("World Bank (Syria's arrears to World Bank Group cleared)", 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/05/16/syria-s-arrears-to-the-world-bank-group-cleared'), ('Curtis LLP (Caesar Act repeal)', 'https://www.curtis.com/our-firm/news/u-s-repeals-the-caesar-act-in-latest-move-to-ease-syria-sanctions'), ("Karamshaar (Syria's external debt figures)", 'https://karamshaar.com/syria-in-figures/syrias-external-debt-how-much-to-whom-is-it-owed-and-will-it-be-repaid/'), ('IMF (Staff Concludes Staff Visit to Syria, Feb 2026)', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/02/25/pr26062-syria-imf-staff-concludes-staff-visit-to-syria')]},
    'TUN': {'summary': "Tunisia's public debt exceeded 80% of GDP in 2025 and is increasingly held domestically rather than externally, following the government's 2024-2025 laws letting the Central Bank of Tunisia directly finance the treasury (up to a combined $4.45bn across 2024-2025) after Moody's (January 2023, downgrade to Caa2) and Fitch (2023, downgrade to CCC-) effectively locked Tunisia out of Eurobond markets amid funding-crunch fears. The EU is overwhelmingly Tunisia's largest trading and investment partner (59.5% of 2025 trade), and it is also Tunisia's main source of budget-support substitute financing via the July 2023 Memorandum of Understanding (~€1bn led by Italy and the Netherlands) tied to migration cooperation, since a roughly $1.9bn IMF Extended Fund Facility agreed in principle in October 2022 has never been finalized — talks stalled after President Kais Saied rejected IMF 'diktats' in April 2023 and refused to commit to subsidy cuts or state-enterprise restructuring, with the World Bank separately suspending new lending after Saied's 2023 remarks on sub-Saharan migrants. Tunisia's main external supporters, absent IMF/Eurobond access, are therefore the EU (migration-linked aid) and, more informally, Algeria (energy and some budget solidarity) rather than the Gulf states that backstop Egypt and Jordan. There is no major bilateral-rivalry tension comparable to Algeria-Morocco, but the structural 'rivalry' is domestic and institutional — between President Saied's centralized, IMF-skeptical governance model and the international financial institutions whose financing Tunisia has effectively forgone since 2022-2023, a stance reaffirmed by Saied's October 2024 re-election.", 'sources': [("Moody's — Tunisia sovereign rating downgrade, January 2023", 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2023/01/28/moodys-downgrades-tunisias-credit-ratings-amid-funding-crunch/'), ('Al Jazeera — Tunisia and EU finalize migration deal, July 2023', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/7/16/tunisia-and-eu-finalise-deal-on-migration'), ("Africanews — Saied rejects IMF 'diktats', April 2023", 'https://www.africanews.com/2023/04/07/tunisias-president-kais-saied-rejects-imf-diktats/'), ("TIMEP — Tunisia's 2025 financial gambles: central bank financing", 'https://timep.org/2025/02/25/tunisias-2025-financial-gambles-taxes-loans-and-the-central-bank/'), ("Carnegie Endowment — Independence usurped: Tunisia's central bank under Saied", 'https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2024/12/independence-usurped-tunisias-central-bank-under-kais-saieds-rule')]},
    'YEM': {'summary': "Yemen has no active IMF financing arrangement: a $552.9m, three-year Extended Credit Facility approved in September 2014 was never fully disbursed and lapsed by March 2016 after the war made it impossible to complete required reviews, and the IMF has since limited engagement to periodic Article IV consultations (the 2025 consultation concluded in early 2026) rather than new lending. Saudi Arabia and the UAE are Yemen's largest bilateral financiers, having deposited billions of dollars over the years directly into the Central Bank of Yemen (Aden) to defend the rial and fund fuel imports, alongside leading the military coalition that has backed the internationally recognized government since 2015. Iran, by contrast, is the Houthi movement's chief external patron, supplying weapons technology Houthi forces have used to attack both Saudi territory and Red Sea shipping, making Tehran-aligned support the central driver of Yemen's continued territorial and monetary fragmentation. China and India are important trade partners as buyers of Yemeni crude when export terminals are operating and as sources of imported goods, but neither plays a significant role in Yemen's fiscal rescue. The World Bank's International Development Association and UN humanitarian agencies effectively substitute for normal sovereign financing given Yemen has had no unified national budget process since the 2014 Houthi takeover. A notable 2025 development was the government's establishment of the National Committee for Regulating Foreign Exchange Institutions (NCRFI) in July 2025, which helped the rial appreciate from roughly 2,900 to 1,600 per dollar within weeks and cut inflation from about 35% to roughly 6% year-on-year by September 2025.", 'sources': [('IMF (Republic of Yemen 2025 Article IV Consultation)', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/downloadpdf/view/journals/002/2026/080/article-A001-en.pdf'), ("World Bank (Economic fragmentation and external shocks hamper Yemen's recovery)", 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/06/02/economic-fragmentation-and-external-shocks-hamper-yemen-s-recovery-path'), ("Sana'a Center for Strategic Studies (Rescuing Yemen's Economy)", 'https://sanaacenter.org/publications/main-publications/23729'), ("Sana'a Center (Oil exports remain halted, Nov 2022)", 'https://sanaacenter.org/the-yemen-review/november-2022/19204'), ('Council on Foreign Relations (Global Conflict Tracker: Yemen)', 'https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/war-yemen')]},
}


# ============================================================
# COUNTRY TRADE & SECTOR PROFILES — main economic sectors, top exports,
# top imports, and leading trade partners for all 26 tracked countries.
# Compiled from well-established, stable economic-geography knowledge
# (the kind found in the CIA World Factbook and the Observatory of
# Economic Complexity / UN Comtrade) rather than a single per-country
# citation — see the Methodology tab for the full source list. Figures
# describing shares (e.g. "~90% of exports") are directional, not
# precise-to-the-decimal statistics.
# ============================================================
COUNTRY_TRADE_PROFILE = {
    'AFG': {'sectors': "Afghanistan's formal economy remains overwhelmingly agrarian and informal, with agriculture (wheat, fruits, nuts, and historically opium poppy) the largest employer and mining (coal, and undeveloped copper/lithium/rare-earth deposits) a long-discussed but largely unrealized source of future revenue. A war-economy of aid-funded services collapsed almost entirely after August 2021, replaced by a smaller trade and transit economy centered on cross-border commerce with Pakistan, Iran, and Central Asia. Formal banking and financial services remain severely constrained by the frozen central-bank reserves and international sanctions-adjacent restrictions on the Taliban government.", 'exports': "Dried and fresh fruits (grapes, pomegranates, apricots), nuts (pine nuts, almonds), and carpets make up the bulk of formal Afghan exports, historically routed heavily through Pakistan but increasingly rerouted through Iran and Central Asia amid the 2025-2026 border conflict. Coal exports to Pakistan have grown as a notable recent revenue source for the Taliban administration. Opium and its derivatives remain a large but officially suppressed and unmeasured component of actual export-equivalent value, notwithstanding the Taliban's 2022 poppy cultivation ban.", 'imports': 'Fuel, wheat and other foodstuffs, machinery, and consumer goods dominate imports, with Afghanistan structurally reliant on imported energy and much of its food security given limited domestic production capacity. Pakistan and Iran have historically been the largest import corridors given geography and lack of Afghan seaport access, though the 2025-2026 border war has forced increased reliance on Iranian and Central Asian (Uzbek, Turkmen) routes. China has also become a more visible source of machinery and consumer imports as the Taliban government has sought alternative partners.', 'partners': "Pakistan and Iran remain Afghanistan's dominant trade partners by sheer geography and historical transit dependence, even as the 2025-2026 border conflict with Pakistan has forced rapid diversification. China has emerged as the most active outside power engaging economically with the Taliban government, particularly around mining concessions, while Gulf states and Russia have provided more limited, ad hoc trade and diplomatic engagement. No Western government maintains a formal aid or trade relationship with the Taliban administration, leaving Afghanistan's formal external economy unusually narrow for a country of its size."},
    'ARE': {'sectors': "The UAE economy is anchored by Abu Dhabi's state oil company ADNOC alongside a large, increasingly diversified non-oil economy centered on Dubai's trade, logistics, tourism, real estate, and financial-services hubs. ADNOC Gas's 2023 IPO and continuing capacity expansion reflect Abu Dhabi's strategy of monetizing hydrocarbon assets through public markets while sovereign investors ADIA, Mubadala, and ADQ redeploy oil wealth into global equities, technology, and overseas development deals such as the 2024 Ras El-Hekma project in Egypt. A new 9% federal corporate tax introduced in June 2023 and continued FATF-driven financial-sector reform mark a shift toward a more conventional fiscal and regulatory base even as free zones retain preferential treatment.", 'exports': "Crude oil and refined petroleum products remain major exports, but the UAE has built one of the region's largest re-export economies through Jebel Ali and Dubai's free zones, with non-oil trade reaching a record roughly AED 3 trillion in 2024. India was the top destination for UAE non-oil exports in 2024 at 13.5% of the total, aided by the 2022 India-UAE CEPA, while gold, jewelry, and refined petroleum products are leading non-oil export categories. The 2022 UAE-Israel CEPA and a widening network of over two dozen CEPA agreements are being used explicitly to diversify export destinations away from oil-price-linked demand.", 'imports': "The UAE imports machinery, electronics, vehicles, and raw materials to supply both domestic consumption and its vast re-export trade, with China as its largest trading partner overall -- non-oil trade with China reached AED 181 billion in H1 2026 alone. Imports also support Dubai's role as a regional distribution and repackaging hub for onward trade into Africa, South Asia, and the wider Gulf. Gold and precious-metal imports are a distinctive feature of UAE trade data, reflecting Dubai's status as a global bullion trading center.", 'partners': "China and India are the UAE's two largest trading partners by non-oil volume, with CEPA agreements accelerating growth in both relationships; Saudi Arabia and Turkey round out the top four. The United States remains a critical security partner and a major destination for ADIA and Mubadala portfolio investment, while the UAE's CEPA with Israel (2022) and normalization under the 2020 Abraham Accords opened a new, if politically sensitive, economic corridor. Egypt has become a flagship destination for UAE outbound sovereign investment following the 2024 Ras El-Hekma deal, part of a broader pattern of UAE capital acting as a regional financial backstop."},
    'BGD': {'sectors': "Ready-made garments (RMG) manufacturing is by far the dominant sector, anchoring roughly 80% of Bangladesh's approximately $48bn in total exports and employing millions, mostly women, across thousands of factories concentrated around Dhaka and Chittagong. Agriculture (rice, jute) and a growing pharmaceutical generics industry are secondary pillars, while remittances from millions of Bangladeshi migrant workers in the Gulf and Southeast Asia provide a critical balance-of-payments cushion alongside garment export earnings. Bangladesh is scheduled to graduate from UN Least Developed Country status in November 2026, which will phase out preferential trade access it has long relied upon.", 'exports': 'RMG exports totalled $39.35bn in FY2024-25 (up 8.84% year-on-year), split between knitwear ($21.16bn) and woven garments ($18.19bn), though growth slowed to roughly 1% year-on-year by FY2025-26 amid political transition uncertainty and looming LDC graduation. The EU absorbs just over half of RMG exports (about $19.7bn) under preferential GSP access, while the US is the largest single national buyer at roughly $7.5bn. Non-RMG exports (leather, jute goods, frozen seafood, pharmaceuticals) remain a small fraction of the total by comparison.', 'imports': "Cotton and other textile raw materials, machinery for the garment sector, fuel, and foodgrains (particularly during production shortfalls) dominate imports, reflecting Bangladesh's dependence on imported inputs to sustain its garment export machine. China is the single largest import source, supplying machinery, fabrics, and industrial inputs. Fertilizer and edible-oil imports have periodically strained the current account during global commodity price spikes.", 'partners': "The European Union and United States are overwhelmingly the most important export destinations given their dominance of RMG demand, while China is the largest import source and a major infrastructure lender. India provides significant land-border trade and, historically, close political ties, though the relationship cooled markedly after Hasina's 2024 ouster and her exile in India, which the interim Yunus government has publicly criticized. The IMF and multilateral lenders (World Bank, ADB) are central to Bangladesh's post-2023 fiscal and reserves management."},
    'BHR': {'sectors': "Bahrain's economy combines a mature but declining oil and gas sector -- about 19% of GDP, down from over 43% in 2000 -- with a well-established financial-services industry and an aluminum-smelting sector built around Aluminium Bahrain (Alba), which alone represents roughly 12% of GDP and is consistently the country's top non-oil export. Manama has long positioned itself as a regional banking and Islamic-finance hub, though this role has narrowed relative to Dubai and Abu Dhabi. Bahrain's persistent structural fiscal deficit -- with public debt reaching an estimated 146.8% of GDP by end-2025 -- makes it the most financially fragile of the GCC economies and the one most dependent on continued support from wealthier neighbors.", 'exports': "Refined petroleum products and aluminum are Bahrain's two leading export categories, with Alba's aluminum output sold roughly 41% domestically and the remainder exported, led by European buyers followed by the Middle East, the Americas, and Asia. Bahrain also re-exports refined products processed at the Bahrain Petroleum Company (Bapco) refinery, drawing increasingly on crude piped in from Saudi Arabia given Bahrain's own declining domestic output. Financial and insurance services, though not captured in merchandise trade data, are a significant invisible export via Bahrain's role as a regional banking center.", 'imports': 'Bahrain imports crude oil (processed through the Bapco refinery), machinery, food, and consumer goods, with Saudi Arabia as its principal trading partner given deep integration via the King Fahd Causeway connecting the two countries. The UAE, United States, and Japan are also significant import sources. Import demand has been constrained in recent years by fiscal consolidation measures, including VAT increases and subsidy reforms tied to the 2018 GCC support package and subsequent austerity rounds.', 'partners': "Saudi Arabia is by far Bahrain's most important economic and political partner, providing crude oil supply, financial support, and -- since 2011 -- direct security backing; the two countries are physically linked by the King Fahd Causeway. The UAE and Kuwait are Bahrain's other principal GCC patrons, having jointly financed the 2018 $10 billion Fiscal Balance Program alongside Saudi Arabia. The United States maintains the US Navy's Fifth Fleet headquarters in Bahrain. Credit-rating agencies S&P, Moody's, and Fitch are effectively central 'stakeholders' in Bahrain's economic story, given how directly sovereign downgrades (to deep sub-investment grade by 2024-2026) shape Bahrain's borrowing costs and reliance on GCC -- rather than pure market -- financing."},
    'BTN': {'sectors': "Hydropower dominates Bhutan's economy and export base, contributing about 63% of total exports and financed almost entirely through Indian loans and grants, with the Punatsangchhu-I and -II projects (delayed roughly seven years and running far over original budgets) the flagship -- and most financially burdensome -- examples. Tourism, historically managed through a high-value, low-volume model with a daily tourist levy (halved to $100 in 2023 to revive arrivals), and services including finance, insurance and real estate are the next-largest contributors. The newly announced Gelephu Mindfulness City special administrative region, unveiled in December 2023, represents Bhutan's most significant attempt at economic diversification beyond hydropower and tourism.", 'exports': "Electricity sold to India accounts for roughly 63% of Bhutan's exports and is effectively Bhutan's only major hard-currency export commodity, making its export base one of the least diversified of any economy in the region. Minor exports include cement, ferrosilicon, and other electricity-intensive industrial products that leverage Bhutan's cheap domestic power. There is essentially no meaningful non-hydropower, non-India export market.", 'imports': "Fuel, machinery, vehicles, and construction materials -- much of it for the hydropower projects themselves -- dominate imports, with India supplying over 80% of Bhutan's total imports. The Ngultrum's peg to the Indian rupee further locks Bhutan's price levels and import costs to Indian monetary conditions. This near-total import dependence on a single partner is a structural vulnerability rarely found even among small landlocked economies.", 'partners': "India is Bhutan's dominant partner across every dimension: main trading partner, primary aid donor, sole market for hydropower exports, and by far the largest creditor, with India-financed hydropower loans making up roughly 60-65% of Bhutan's external debt (equivalent to over half of GDP). Bhutan's total public debt stood at about 100.5% of GDP as of June 2025 per the IMF's 2025 Article IV consultation (concluded January 2026), almost entirely hydropower-related and owed to India. Bhutan has never had a full IMF lending program, relying instead on periodic Article IV surveillance, and has no significant relationship with China, with which it does not maintain formal diplomatic relations -- leaving it uniquely insulated from the India-China competitive dynamics that shape Nepal, Sri Lanka and the Maldives."},
    'DZA': {'sectors': "Algeria's economy is overwhelmingly hydrocarbon-dependent: oil and natural gas, produced via state company Sonatrach (which controls roughly 80% of hydrocarbon operations), account for about 98% of total exports, roughly 58% of government revenue, and around a quarter to a third of GDP. Outside hydrocarbons, agriculture and a heavily protected domestic manufacturing/import-substitution sector (regulated under the 2022 Investment Law and residual 51/49 foreign-ownership rules in 'strategic sectors') remain underdeveloped, leaving Algeria's fiscal position almost entirely a function of global oil and gas prices.", 'exports': "Hydrocarbon exports — pipeline and LNG natural gas plus crude oil and refined products — dominate overwhelmingly, with Italy alone absorbing over 60% of Algerian gas exports via the Transmed pipeline after a 2022 Eni-Sonatrach deal to raise volumes toward 9 billion cubic meters/year by 2024-25 in the wake of Europe's post-Ukraine-war pivot away from Russian gas. Non-hydrocarbon exports remain marginal by comparison, though Algeria has sought to diversify into phosphate-based fertilizer exports via the new $7bn Bled El Hadba megaproject launched with Chinese partners in 2024.", 'imports': "Algeria imports capital goods, machinery, pharmaceuticals, and consumer goods, with China having overtaken France as its largest single source of imports (Chinese exports to Algeria reached $11.68bn in 2024). Import substitution policy — including 51/49 domestic-ownership rules for importers of goods for domestic resale under the 2021 Finance Law — reflects a longstanding government effort to reduce Algeria's reliance on imported manufactured goods and preserve foreign-currency reserves built from hydrocarbon windfalls.", 'partners': "Italy is Algeria's most important trade partner by value given its gas dependence, while China has become the dominant source of imports and infrastructure financing (Belt and Road projects including the completed 1,216km East-West highway, worth a cumulative $23.85bn in Chinese investment/contracts 2005-2020). France, Algeria's former colonial power, remains economically important but politically fraught — ties ruptured badly in 2024-2025 over France's Western Sahara policy shift and the Boualem Sansal case — while Morocco, its historic rival, has had no formal diplomatic or land-border relationship since 2021 (border closed since 1994) over the Western Sahara dispute."},
    'EGY': {'sectors': "Egypt's economy rests on five pillars: natural gas production (led by the offshore Zohr field and state firm EGAS/EGPC), Suez Canal transit fees, tourism (Red Sea and Nile Valley, boosted by the November 2025 opening of the $1bn Grand Egyptian Museum), remittances from an estimated 10+ million Egyptians abroad, and a state- and military-linked construction/manufacturing sector (via bodies like the National Service Projects Organization) building mega-projects such as the New Administrative Capital. Agriculture (cotton, rice, citrus) and a growing IT/BPO services sector round out the base, but external financing needs remain acute whenever any of the FX-earning pillars — canal fees or tourism especially — is disrupted by regional conflict.", 'exports': "Goods exports are led by crude oil and refined petroleum products, natural gas/LNG (from Idku and Damietta terminals when domestic demand allows a surplus), ready-made garments and textiles, and agricultural products like citrus and potatoes, but Egypt's most important 'exports' are arguably services: Suez Canal transit fees (which fell roughly 50% y/y by early 2026 due to Houthi Red Sea attacks, from about $800m/month at peak to roughly $380m/month) and tourism receipts. Large one-off FDI/real-estate deals such as the 2024 $35bn Ras El-Hekma agreement with the UAE's ADQ and the roughly $29.7bn Qatari Diar framework now function as quasi-export financing events that periodically transform the balance of payments overnight.", 'imports': "Egypt is one of the world's largest wheat importers, historically reliant on Russian and Ukrainian grain (a dependency exposed sharply by the 2022 invasion of Ukraine), alongside refined fuel products, machinery, and vehicles. Because so much of the import bill (food, fuel, capital goods) is inelastic, Egypt's current account and FX reserves are highly sensitive to global commodity-price swings and to the volume/cost of dollar remittance inflows, which hit a record $47.3bn in FY2025/26 after the March 2024 devaluation narrowed the parallel-market gap that had previously diverted remittances to informal channels.", 'partners': "Egypt's principal trade partners are the EU (Italy and Germany prominent), China, the United States, and the Gulf states, but Gulf countries — the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Qatar, and Kuwait — matter more as direct investors and central-bank depositors than as trade counterparties, exemplified by the 2024 $35bn Ras El-Hekma deal and the roughly $9.3bn in standing Gulf deposits at the Central Bank of Egypt (Saudi Arabia $5.3bn, Kuwait $4.0bn) as of mid-2024. This investor relationship has effectively displaced part of Egypt's traditional reliance on IMF financing alone, with Gulf capital and the IMF's $8bn EFF (seventh review completed July 2026, unlocking $1.8bn) now operating as complementary rather than sequential sources of external support."},
    'IND': {'sectors': "Services -- particularly IT and business-process outsourcing, software, and a rapidly growing digital/fintech sector -- are the largest single driver of India's export economy, alongside a globally significant generics pharmaceutical industry and expanding electronics and defense manufacturing under 'Make in India' policies. Agriculture still employs a plurality of the workforce despite contributing a shrinking share of GDP, while automotive, textiles, and gems/jewelry remain major traditional manufacturing exports. India's economy is now the fastest-growing among major economies, expanding 7.7% in 2025 despite steep US tariff pressure.", 'exports': "Services exports reached roughly $421.3bn in FY2025-26 (up from about $387.5bn the prior year), led by telecommunications/computer/information services and business services, making India one of the world's largest services exporters. Pharmaceutical exports rose to $30.47bn in FY2024-25 -- more than double the FY2013-14 level -- with the US the single largest market for both IT services and generic drugs. Total exports (goods plus services) reached a record $824.9bn in FY2024-25, though the IMF projected merchandise exports could fall roughly 5.8% in FY2025-26 to about $416bn under tariff pressure before a February 2026 US-India trade deal eased the outlook.", 'imports': "Crude oil and petroleum products are India's single largest import category by value, reflecting heavy energy-import dependence, followed by gold, electronics, and machinery. Goods imports were projected by the IMF to rise about 2.4% in FY2025-26 to roughly $746bn. China remains a major source of electronics, machinery, and industrial inputs despite the post-Galwan political chill, underscoring the gap between strategic tension and continued deep manufacturing-supply-chain reliance.", 'partners': "The United States is India's largest single trading partner and the dominant market for both IT services and pharmaceutical exports, making the 2025 US tariff escalation (to 25%, later reduced to about 18% under a February 2026 deal) an unusually consequential bilateral economic event. Mauritius and Singapore are the largest sources of foreign direct investment (each accounting for roughly a quarter of India's cumulative FDI inflows, which totalled $81.04bn in FY2024-25), largely reflecting historical tax-treaty structuring rather than underlying economic ties. China is simultaneously a major trade partner by volume and India's most significant strategic rival, particularly following the 2020 Galwan Valley border clash, while Pakistan tension escalated sharply with the April-May 2025 Pahalgam attack and Operation Sindoor conflict."},
    'IRN': {'sectors': "Iran's economy remains heavily hydrocarbon-dependent despite decades of sanctions pressure, with oil and gas historically supplying a large share of export earnings and government revenue even as sanctions have repeatedly slashed export volumes; a parallel 'resistance economy' of import-substitution industry, petrochemicals, and a large informal/sanctions-evasion sector has developed to cushion the formal economy. The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) controls a substantial share of construction, telecoms and industrial conglomerates through its business arms, blurring the line between state, military and commercial activity. The February 2026 war with the US and Israel delivered the most severe shock to this structure in the Islamic Republic's history, driving crude exports to zero by May 2026 and shrinking GDP an estimated 6.1% for the year.", 'exports': "Crude oil is Iran's dominant export, sold almost entirely to China -- both directly and via so-called 'teapot' independent refineries concentrated in Shandong province, which by 2025 processed an estimated 90% of Iran's total oil exports -- using a shadow fleet of tankers and ship-to-ship transfers to disguise the crude's origin as Omani, Iraqi, Russian or Malaysian-blend oil. Petrochemicals, natural gas (piped to Iraq and Turkey), and non-oil goods such as agricultural products, carpets and steel make up a smaller but still sanctions-constrained share of exports. The February 2026 war cut Iranian oil exports from roughly 2.1 million bpd to zero within three months, eliminating the country's primary source of hard currency.", 'imports': "Iran imports machinery, industrial equipment, foodstuffs and consumer goods largely through Chinese, Russian, Turkish, UAE and Iraqi intermediaries, given its exclusion from most Western supply chains and the SWIFT financial messaging system since 2018. The country has developed extensive barter, informal-exchange-house and hawala-style payment networks to route trade around sanctions, particularly for pharmaceuticals and food (nominally sanctions-exempt but frequently obstructed by international banks' reluctance to process any Iran-linked payment). Since the September 2025 UN snapback and the February 2026 war, import channels have narrowed further, worsening shortages of medicine and industrial inputs alongside the currency collapse.", 'partners': "China is by far Iran's most important economic partner, absorbing an estimated 90% of Iran's crude exports through direct purchases and teapot refineries, and serving as a key supplier of manufactured goods and infrastructure investment under a 2021, 25-year cooperation agreement. Russia is Iran's principal military and diplomatic partner, jointly resisting the 2025 E3-triggered UN snapback sanctions alongside China at the Security Council. Iran's adversarial relationships -- the United States, Israel, and the E3 European powers -- define its sanctions exposure and, since February 2026, direct military conflict; the UAE and Oman serve as important, more neutral commercial and diplomatic intermediaries, illustrated by the August 2026 Iran-Oman agreement on a temporary Strait of Hormuz transit corridor."},
    'IRQ': {'sectors': "Iraq's economy remains overwhelmingly hydrocarbon-dependent: crude oil and refined products account for roughly 90% of government revenue and a large share of GDP, produced almost entirely by state-owned entities under the Ministry of Oil, including the Basra Oil Company and Kurdistan's semi-autonomous producers. Non-oil sectors -- agriculture (wheat, dates, barley), construction, and a small manufacturing base -- remain underdeveloped after decades of war, sanctions and underinvestment, together employing much of the workforce but contributing a far smaller share of output. The 2026 Iran-Israel-US war, which cut national oil output from roughly 4.4 million bpd to under 900,000 bpd by June 2026, starkly exposed this lack of diversification.", 'exports': "Crude oil, marketed almost exclusively through the State Organization for Marketing of Oil (SOMO), constitutes over 99% of Iraq's export earnings; Iraq exported more than 1.2 billion barrels in 2024, generating over $71bn from Asian buyers alone. China and India are the dominant destinations, together purchasing around 60% of Iraqi crude in 2024 (China roughly 1.19 million bpd, India about 979,000 bpd), with most volumes moving via Gulf terminals and the remainder through the Turkish port of Ceyhan. A 2024 budget amendment compensating international oil companies operating in Kurdistan at $16/barrel was designed to help restart halted KRG exports through Ceyhan.", 'imports': "Iraq imports the bulk of its consumer goods, machinery, vehicles, foodstuffs and refined petroleum products, reflecting a domestic manufacturing base hollowed out by decades of conflict and sanctions. Turkey and Iran are leading suppliers of food, construction materials and, in Iran's case, electricity and natural gas that Baghdad depends on to keep its power grid running -- a dependence Washington has periodically restricted via sanctions-waiver policy. Reconstruction-related imports of building materials and equipment have also grown as Iraq works to rebuild Mosul, Fallujah and other cities devastated during the 2014-2017 war against ISIS.", 'partners': "China and India are Iraq's most important trade partners as top oil buyers, while Turkey and Iran are critical for overland trade and electricity/gas imports (and, in Turkey's case, the Kurdistan pipeline route). The United States remains central to Iraq's financial plumbing through the Federal Reserve Bank of New York's oversight of Iraq's dollar reserves and tightened controls since November 2022 on Iraqi banks to curb dollar smuggling to Iran. Gulf states, particularly the UAE, have also expanded investment and trade ties with Baghdad since 2021 as part of Iraq's efforts to diversify away from Iranian economic dependence."},
    'ISR': {'sectors': "Israel's economy is dominated by a globally integrated high-tech sector -- software, semiconductors, cybersecurity, and life sciences -- which accounted for about 17.3% of GDP in 2024 and has historically drawn roughly 90% of its venture funding from foreign investors. The sector proved highly sensitive to domestic political risk, with startup funding falling nearly 70% during the 2023 judicial-reform crisis, and to war-related disruption, with exports and investment both contracting sharply after the October 2023 Gaza war began. Traditional sectors -- diamonds, chemicals, agriculture, and defense manufacturing (Elbit Systems, Israel Aerospace Industries, Rafael) -- remain significant, with defense exports benefiting from wartime demand even as broader consumption and investment activity has been repeatedly disrupted by the 2023-2025 multi-front conflicts.", 'exports': "High-tech exports reached about $78 billion in 2024, of which 72% was software services rather than hardware, and climbed to a record 57.2% of Israel's total exports in early 2025. Traditional exports -- polished diamonds, chemicals, and agricultural products -- remain significant but represent a shrinking share relative to technology and defense-related exports. Defense exports have risen further amid the 2023-2025 wars, even as the broader Q4 2023 export contraction of 18.3% illustrated how directly war disrupts non-defense trade.", 'imports': "Israel imports energy, machinery, vehicles, and consumer goods, alongside substantial US-funded military hardware under the 2019-2028 Memorandum of Understanding providing $3.8 billion annually in security assistance ($3.3 billion in Foreign Military Financing plus $500 million for missile defense). Import volumes and private consumption both fell sharply during the acute war phases -- private consumption dropped 26.9% in Q4 2023 alone -- before partially recovering. The US remains Israel's single most important supplier of advanced defense equipment, complemented by domestic co-production arrangements with Elbit and IAI.", 'partners': "The United States is Israel's most important economic and security partner by a wide margin, providing $3.8 billion annually in military aid under the 2019-2028 MOU and serving as a key mediator (alongside Qatar and Egypt) of the 2023-2025 Gaza ceasefires. The European Union collectively is a major trading partner and a primary destination for Israeli high-tech and industrial exports. The UAE, following the 2020 Abraham Accords and 2022 CEPA, became Israel's most significant new Arab economic partner, though the relationship has been strained since October 2023. Iran is Israel's principal existential adversary, a rivalry that escalated to direct state-on-state conflict in the 2024 exchanges and the 12-day Operation Rising Lion war of June 2025, with Iranian-backed Hezbollah and Hamas representing the immediate proxy dimensions of that conflict."},
    'JOR': {'sectors': "Jordan's economy is built on phosphate and potash mining (Jordan Phosphate Mines Company and Arab Potash Company, exploiting the world's fifth-largest phosphate reserves), tourism (Petra, the Dead Sea, Wadi Rum, and Aqaba), remittances from Jordanians working in the Gulf, and substantial foreign aid — the U.S. alone provides roughly $845m/year in budget and security support under a 2022 seven-year MOU. A large, aid-dependent public sector and persistent double-digit unemployment (with youth unemployment far higher) reflect the economy's structural fragility, which is compounded by hosting one of the world's largest per-capita refugee populations since the Syrian civil war began in 2011.", 'exports': "Potash exports (via Arab Potash Company, over 2.1 million tonnes annually to India, China, Indonesia, Egypt and others) and phosphate rock exports (via JPMC, mainly to India at 63%, Turkey at 26%, and Iraq) are Jordan's leading goods exports, alongside pharmaceuticals and garments produced under U.S. free-trade Qualifying Industrial Zone arrangements. A planned joint industrial venture between APC and JPMC aims to move Jordan up the value chain into fertilizer derivatives rather than raw mineral exports.", 'imports': "Jordan imports the bulk of its energy needs and a wide range of consumer and capital goods, with roughly one-third of imports transiting the Red Sea via the port of Aqaba — a chokepoint whose 2023-2024 disruption by Houthi attacks (container volumes fell from 148,454 in Q1 2023 to 121,843 in Q1 2024) directly raised import costs and consumer prices. This exposure to Red Sea shipping security is a distinguishing structural vulnerability relative to Egypt's canal-fee exposure or Morocco/Tunisia's Mediterranean-facing trade routes.", 'partners': "The United States is Jordan's most important strategic and financial partner, providing the largest per-capita U.S. foreign assistance outside Israel (~$845m/year under the 2022 MOU) in exchange for Jordan's role as a security anchor bordering Syria, Iraq, and Israel/Palestine; Gulf states (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Kuwait) have periodically provided large aid packages (e.g. a $2.5bn pledge in 2018) and remain top destinations for Jordanian potash exports and expatriate labor generating remittances. The IMF (current $1.2bn EFF approved January 2024, fifth review completed June 2026) and the World Bank (a further $1.1bn in financing announced in 2025) round out Jordan's external-support architecture, while its main risk exposure is geopolitical rather than bilateral-rivalry-driven — spillover from the Gaza war (2023-2024) and the Israel-Iran war (2025-2026) rather than any direct territorial dispute."},
    'KWT': {'sectors': "Kuwait's economy remains among the most oil-dependent in the Gulf: hydrocarbons contribute roughly half of GDP and about 90% of both export earnings and government fiscal revenue, produced upstream by Kuwait Oil Company and refined downstream by Kuwait National Petroleum Company, both subsidiaries of state holding company Kuwait Petroleum Corporation (KPC). KPC's roughly $16 billion Al-Zour refinery, which reached full 615,000 barrel-per-day capacity in February 2024, lifted Kuwait's total refining capacity to about 1.415 million bpd. Non-oil sectors -- banking, real estate, and a small manufacturing base -- have grown slowly, constrained for years by political gridlock between the government and the (now-suspended) National Assembly that delayed reform legislation, including the debt law finally passed in 2025.", 'exports': "Crude oil and refined petroleum products make up roughly 90% of Kuwait's export earnings, sold internationally through KPC's marketing arm to customers concentrated in Asia, particularly China, South Korea, Japan, and India. KPC has targeted raising oil production capacity to 4 million barrels per day by 2035 and natural gas output to 4 billion cubic feet per day by 2030. Non-oil exports remain a very small share of the total, reflecting limited industrial diversification relative to Gulf peers such as the UAE or Saudi Arabia.", 'imports': "Kuwait imports machinery, vehicles, foodstuffs, and construction materials to support both consumption and infrastructure projects, including materials tied to the Al-Zour refinery buildout and other KPC downstream investments. As a small, wealthy, import-dependent consumer market, Kuwait sources significant volumes of food and consumer goods from the US, EU, China, and neighboring GCC states via re-export hubs in the UAE. Capital-goods imports for the oil and gas sector form a persistent, cyclical component of the import bill tied to KPC's capacity-expansion timeline.", 'partners': "China, South Korea, Japan, and India are Kuwait's most important oil-export markets, reflecting Asia's dominant share of Gulf crude demand. The United States remains Kuwait's key security partner, a relationship rooted in the 1990-91 Gulf War liberation, hosting US forces at Camp Arifjan. Within the GCC, Saudi Arabia and the UAE are Kuwait's closest political and economic partners, including on OPEC+ production coordination and joint management of the shared Neutral Zone oilfields with Saudi Arabia. Kuwait's 2025 return to international bond markets, after an eight-year absence enabled by the political suspension of parliament, opened relationships with a broad base of global institutional bond investors as a new source of financing alongside its sovereign wealth reserves."},
    'LBN': {'sectors': "Lebanon's economy has historically centered on banking and financial services, tourism, real estate and large diaspora-remittance inflows rather than manufacturing or resource extraction; the collapse of the banking sector since 2019 -- with roughly $72bn in deposits effectively trapped -- has hollowed out what was once the country's leading sector. Agriculture (fruit, vegetables, wine) and light manufacturing (food processing, chemicals, jewelry, textiles) persist at small scale, while remittances from a large diaspora remain one of the economy's few stable hard-currency inflows even amid the crisis. Post-war reconstruction in the south, if financed, would add a construction-driven growth sector alongside the pre-existing services base.", 'exports': "Lebanon's formal goods exports are modest relative to its historical role as a regional trade and financial hub, dominated by jewelry and precious metals, processed foods, chemicals and paper products, mostly destined for Gulf states, Syria, Iraq and other Arab markets via the small but historically important Port of Beirut, whose capacity was crippled by the 2020 explosion. Re-exports and informal Syrian border trade (fuel, goods) have long been economically significant but poorly captured in official statistics. Reconstruction of the south and any revival of confidence in Beirut's port and banking system are widely seen as prerequisites for a meaningful expansion of goods exports.", 'imports': "Lebanon imports the vast majority of its fuel, wheat, pharmaceuticals and consumer goods, a dependence starkly exposed when the 2020 Beirut port explosion destroyed the country's main import gateway and central grain silo. Since the 2019 crisis, chronic dollar shortages have forced the central bank to ration subsidies for fuel, medicine and wheat imports, fueling shortages and inflation; fuel imports from Iraq and Gulf-funded fuel shipments have periodically plugged supply gaps. A 2025 Gulf-backed economic development zone proposal for south Lebanon would, if realized, bring new construction-material and equipment imports.", 'partners': "Gulf states -- historically Saudi Arabia and Qatar, alongside the UAE -- have been Lebanon's most consequential external economic partners, providing deposits, investment and, since 2025, pledges of reconstruction financing conditioned on Hezbollah's disarmament. The United States plays an outsized role both as sanctions enforcer (targeting Hezbollah's financial networks) and as broker of the November 2024 Israel-Lebanon ceasefire and subsequent disarmament diplomacy. Foreign Eurobond holders -- an estimated 40% of at least one defaulted note per Oxford Economics -- represent Lebanon's most direct financial-market creditor relationship, still unresolved five years after the March 2020 default. Iran, via Hezbollah, remains Lebanon's most fraught geopolitical relationship, central to both its security exposure to Israel and its difficulty attracting Western and Gulf investment."},
    'LBY': {'sectors': "Libya's economy is almost totally hydrocarbon-dependent: oil and gas historically supply around 95-96% of government revenue and the large majority of GDP and export earnings, produced through the state-owned National Oil Corporation (NOC) in partnership with foreign majors including Italy's Eni, France's TotalEnergies and US-based ConocoPhillips. Non-oil sectors -- construction, small-scale agriculture, and a bloated public-sector employment base -- remain underdeveloped and heavily subsidized, a structure largely unchanged since the Gaddafi era despite 15 years of conflict. Political fragmentation between rival Tripoli- and eastern-based administrations has repeatedly translated into oil-field shutdowns and central-bank disputes that directly transmit political risk into hydrocarbon output.", 'exports': "Crude oil and, increasingly, natural gas dominate Libyan exports, marketed through the National Oil Corporation; Libya became Italy's single largest crude oil supplier in 2025, shipping 13.4 million tonnes (nearly a quarter of Italy's oil imports) ahead of Azerbaijan, Kazakhstan and Iraq. A January 2026, 25-year agreement with TotalEnergies and ConocoPhillips aims to more than double output at the Waha concessions toward roughly 850,000 bpd, while Eni's Mellitah Oil & Gas joint venture is expanding gas exports to Italy via the Greenstream pipeline. Export volumes, however, remain hostage to recurring blockades: the 2020 and 2022 shutdowns each briefly cut national output by half or more.", 'imports': "Libya imports the majority of its food, consumer goods, machinery and refined fuel products (despite being a crude producer, it has limited domestic refining capacity), reflecting an economy that never diversified beyond hydrocarbon extraction. Turkey has become a major supplier of construction materials, machinery and consumer goods, particularly to the Tripoli-aligned west, reflecting Ankara's military and political backing of the GNU since 2019-2020. Reconstruction-related imports of building materials and heavy equipment have also grown in the wake of the September 2023 Derna floods, complicated by the divided governments' separate, uncoordinated procurement processes.", 'partners': "Italy is Libya's most important economic partner, both as the top destination for Libyan crude (its single-largest supplier in 2025) and gas via the Greenstream pipeline, and as home to Eni, Libya's leading foreign oil and gas operator since 1959. France's TotalEnergies and the US's ConocoPhillips are also major upstream investors, having signed a 25-year Waha concessions agreement with the NOC in January 2026. Turkey is Libya's most significant political-economic partner on the western side, providing military backing to the Tripoli government alongside growing trade ties, while the UAE and Egypt back the eastern administration -- Libya's foreign economic relationships largely mirror its internal political split. Russia has also been an active but more informal financial actor, having printed billions of dinars for the eastern central bank branch between 2015 and 2020 via its state printer Goznak."},
    'LKA': {'sectors': "Tourism, tea, apparel manufacturing, and remittances from overseas workers form the four pillars of Sri Lanka's post-crisis recovery, with services contributing around 59% of GDP and over 48% of employment. Tourism earnings reached an estimated $3.17bn in 2024 (up 53% from 2023), with arrivals surpassing one million by May 2025, while Sri Lanka remained the world's largest tea exporter by volume in 2024. The economy has recovered roughly 40% of the GDP lost during the 2018-2023 crisis as of end-2024, supported by the completed debt restructuring and ongoing IMF program.", 'exports': "Apparel/ready-made-garment exports grew 5.2% to reach $4.9bn in 2025, remaining Sri Lanka's largest single merchandise export category, while tea exports go predominantly to Iraq, Russia, Turkey, Iran and the UAE rather than to neighboring South Asian markets. Total exports reached roughly $12.7bn in 2024 (up from $11.9bn in 2023), with export earnings hitting a record $8bn in just the first half of 2025. Rubber products, coconut-based goods, and gems/jewelry make up most of the remainder.", 'imports': 'Refined petroleum, textile inputs for the apparel sector, machinery, and foodgrains dominate imports, with fuel imports having been the proximate trigger of the 2022 crisis when reserves ran out to pay for them. China and India are both significant sources of industrial and consumer imports, while India in particular supplies substantial fuel and food via credit lines extended during the 2022 crisis. Specialty and blending teas are imported from China, India and Kenya even as Sri Lanka exports its own tea in bulk.', 'partners': "China and Japan are Sri Lanka's largest bilateral official creditors alongside the Paris Club-adjacent Official Creditor Committee (co-chaired by India, Japan and France), all central to the 2023-2024 debt restructuring. India has positioned itself as both a first-responder creditor (emergency credit lines in 2022) and Sri Lanka's most geographically embedded economic partner, competing directly with China for infrastructure and port influence -- the Hambantota port lease-to-China remains the emblematic flashpoint of that competition. The IMF anchors the overall macroeconomic framework through its Extended Fund Facility, with the Fund's third review completed in February 2025 and a fifth/sixth combined review staff-level agreement reached in April 2026."},
    'MAR': {'sectors': "Morocco's economy has diversified furthest among Maghreb peers, anchored by phosphate mining and fertilizer production via state-controlled OCP Group (holding over 70% of world phosphate reserves and a 31% global market share), a rapidly expanding automotive manufacturing cluster (Renault's Tangier/Casablanca plants and Stellantis's Kenitra facility, which Stellantis is expanding via a €1.2bn investment toward 535,000 vehicles/year by 2030), tourism, agriculture, and remittances. A nascent EV battery/gigafactory industry (led by a CDG-Gotion joint venture) and aerospace supply-chain manufacturing add further diversification, though the country remains exposed to European demand cycles and, as the 2023 Al Haouz earthquake and chronic drought show, to natural-resource and climate shocks.", 'exports': "Automotive exports are now Morocco's largest single export category, hitting a record 157.6 billion dirhams (~$16bn) in 2024 before dipping slightly in 2025 and rebounding 18.6% y/y in early 2026; OCP's phosphate and fertilizer exports are the second pillar, with 2024 fertilizer export volumes reaching a record 12.37 million tonnes (+13% y/y) and phosphate-related export revenue up 20.9% y/y through July 2025 amid a global fertilizer price boom. Textiles, agri-food exports (citrus, seafood), and tourism receipts round out Morocco's external earnings base.", 'imports': 'Morocco imports energy (it has limited domestic oil/gas), industrial inputs and components for its automotive and electronics assembly plants, machinery, and wheat, with import needs closely tied to the pace of its manufacturing-sector expansion. The government has run Eurobond issuances (including a €2bn sale in 2025, its first since 2020) partly to fund import-intensive World Cup 2030 infrastructure and post-earthquake reconstruction (a MAD 120bn/~$11.7bn five-year program) without excessive current-account strain.', 'partners': "The EU is Morocco's dominant trading partner, accounting for 33.7% of goods trade in 2025, with Spain as the single largest bilateral partner (€22.7bn in 2025 trade) followed by France, which is also Morocco's leading bilateral sovereign lender ahead of Germany, Japan, and Spain. Beyond Europe, Morocco has cultivated deepening Gulf financial ties and a fast-growing Chinese manufacturing-investment relationship (the Gotion EV battery gigafactory, initially ~$1.3bn scaling toward $6.5bn), while its central geopolitical fault line remains the Western Sahara dispute with Algeria, on which Morocco has secured successive diplomatic wins — U.S. recognition (2020), Spain's reversal (2022), and France's shift (2024) — that have progressively isolated Algiers's position."},
    'MDV': {'sectors': "Tourism is overwhelmingly the largest sector, directly contributing almost 30% of GDP and generating over 60% of foreign-currency earnings through a network of luxury resort islands. Fishing (particularly tuna) is the second traditional pillar and a major source of formal employment outside the capital region, though it is dwarfed by tourism in dollar terms. Construction, much of it tied to Chinese- and Indian-financed infrastructure projects (bridges, airports, housing), has grown rapidly but has also been the primary driver of the country's ballooning external debt.", 'exports': "Tourism receipts function as the Maldives' de facto dominant 'export,' overshadowing recorded merchandise exports, which consist mainly of fresh and processed fish (tuna) products sold regionally and to the EU and Asian markets. Formal merchandise export volumes remain small relative to the size of the tourism economy. A 2024 diplomatic rift with India cut Indian tourist arrivals by roughly 42%, directly costing the tourism-dependent economy an estimated $2bn -- illustrating how concentrated the country's hard-currency earnings are in a single sector and, historically, a single major source market.", 'imports': "Nearly all food, fuel, construction materials, and consumer goods are imported given the country's tiny land area and lack of domestic manufacturing or agriculture, making the Maldives acutely exposed to global commodity price swings and shipping disruptions. Construction materials and machinery tied to Chinese-financed infrastructure projects (bridges, the Male-Hulhule connection, airport expansion) have been a particularly large and debt-generating import category since the mid-2010s. Fuel imports are a chronic drain on scarce foreign-exchange reserves, which fell below $65 million in usable terms by December 2024.", 'partners': "China and India are simultaneously Maldives' largest creditors and its two most actively competing geopolitical patrons: China holds roughly $1.3bn of external debt (with a 2025 bilateral arrangement deferring repayments five years) against India's roughly $130 million, though India has separately extended a $554 million line of credit and reduced Maldives' annual debt-service burden to India by 40% in 2025. President Muizzu's 2023 election on an 'India Out' platform initially favored Beijing, but the subsequent 2024 #BoycottMaldives fallout and the deepening debt crisis forced a partial pivot back toward New Delhi through 2024-2025. The Maldives has never entered a full IMF program -- relying on Article IV surveillance (most recently concluded June 2026) plus ad hoc Chinese and Indian bilateral relief -- reflecting a deliberate strategy of avoiding IMF conditionality by leveraging great-power competition for its debt management instead."},
    'NPL': {'sectors': "Remittances from an estimated several million Nepali migrant workers abroad (mostly in the Gulf, Malaysia and India) constitute the single largest external resource, at roughly 26-27% of GDP in recent years and reaching an all-time monthly high above Rs 200 billion in November 2025. Hydropower has rapidly emerged as the second major growth pillar, with Nepal's export capacity to India climbing past 1,000 MW by 2025 and a first-ever export to Bangladesh (40 MW) begun in June 2025. Tourism and small-scale agriculture round out an economy still classified as least-developed, though 2025's Gen-Z protests and political transition disrupted both sectors materially.", 'exports': "Nepal's formal merchandise exports remain small relative to its economy, dominated by hydroelectricity sales to India under the January 2024 ten-year, 10,000 MW power-trade agreement, alongside textiles, carpets, and agricultural goods such as cardamom and tea. Tourism receipts, while not counted as merchandise exports, are a critical foreign-currency earner that took a roughly $178 million hit from the September 2025 Gen-Z protests. The country runs a persistently large trade deficit financed almost entirely by remittance inflows.", 'imports': "Petroleum products, machinery, vehicles, and manufactured consumer goods dominate imports, nearly all routed through India given Nepal's landlocked geography and near-total transit dependence on Indian ports and highways. This dependence was the central vulnerability exposed by the 2015-2016 unofficial Indian blockade, when roughly 2,200 industries shut down over a constitutional dispute. China has slowly expanded as an alternative import and infrastructure-financing source, though transit-route limitations keep India structurally dominant.", 'partners': "India is overwhelmingly Nepal's most important economic partner -- the destination for hydropower exports, the source of the vast majority of imports, and the sole practical transit route to the sea -- a dependence periodically weaponized, as in 2015-2016. China is Nepal's principal counterweight, courted for infrastructure investment and as a check on Indian leverage, though its practical economic footprint remains far smaller than India's. The IMF has anchored Nepal's macro-fiscal framework via a $395.9 million Extended Credit Facility approved in January 2022, extended in December 2025 after its sixth review, providing continuity through the 2025 political upheaval."},
    'OMN': {'sectors': "Oman's economy remains oil- and gas-dependent, though less so than other Gulf states, with hydrocarbons produced by Petroleum Development Oman (PDO, majority state-owned) and a smaller downstream refining and petrochemical sector. Since the 2020 oil-price shock, the government's Medium-Term Fiscal Plan and Vision 2040 have pushed diversification into logistics (the Duqm and Sohar ports and special economic zones), green hydrogen, mining, and tourism, alongside a public-debt reduction from about 68% of GDP in 2020 to roughly 34% by end-2024 that helped Oman regain investment-grade credit ratings from S&P, Fitch, and Moody's in 2024-2025. Non-oil GDP growth has consistently outpaced hydrocarbon growth in recent years, reflecting the diversification push.", 'exports': "Crude oil and, increasingly, liquefied natural gas remain Oman's largest export earners, sold primarily to Asian buyers including China, South Korea, and Japan. Non-oil exports -- chemicals, metals, and machinery -- reached about RO 6.2 billion in 2024, a growing but still secondary share of total exports, supported by output from the Duqm and Sohar industrial zones. Oman has also pursued green hydrogen and renewable-energy export projects as a longer-term diversification strategy beyond conventional hydrocarbons.", 'imports': "Oman's imports totaled about $43 billion in 2024, up 12% year-on-year, with food and beverages the largest category at 43% of the total, followed by construction and electrical materials tied to continued infrastructure investment in Duqm, Sohar, and Muscat. The UAE is Oman's largest import source, supplying about 23% of total imports, reflecting deep logistics and re-export integration between the two neighbors; China and Kuwait are also significant suppliers.", 'partners': "The UAE is Oman's most important non-oil trade partner, ranked as its top partner for non-oil exports and re-exports in 2025 and its largest single import source. Saudi Arabia, Qatar, India, South Korea, Japan, and China round out Oman's principal trading relationships, with Asian buyers dominating oil and gas offtake. China is also Oman's most significant strategic investment partner through the roughly $10.7 billion Oman Wanfang-led Duqm Special Economic Zone project. Oman has historically maintained a more neutral foreign policy than its GCC neighbors, including quieter channels to Iran, a diplomatic flexibility that has generally supported rather than hindered its economic relationships across the region."},
    'PAK': {'sectors': "Textiles and apparel dominate Pakistan's export economy, contributing over half of total merchandise exports (55.83% of exports, $17.9bn, in FY2024-25) and anchored by major hubs in Faisalabad and Karachi. Agriculture (cotton, rice, wheat) underpins the textile value chain and remains a major employer, while a growing IT and business-process-outsourcing sector and remittance-driven services increasingly supplement traditional exports. Energy generation, cement, and a fragile banking sector round out the domestically oriented parts of the economy.", 'exports': 'Textiles and garments remain overwhelmingly dominant, with the textile group alone accounting for over 60% of total exports in some recent months of FY2025-26 even as overall textile export growth slowed to roughly 1.25% year-on-year. Rice, leather goods, surgical instruments, and sporting goods make up most of the remainder. The sector has shown resilience through 2024-2025 despite floods and regional trade disruption, though food exports fell sharply (down 35%) in FY2025-26 amid the broader Afghanistan-border trade collapse.', 'imports': "Petroleum products and crude oil, machinery, and palm oil form the largest import categories, reflecting Pakistan's chronic energy-import dependence and limited domestic refining and edible-oil capacity. Chemicals, fertilizer inputs, and iron/steel for construction are also significant, while Chinese machinery and consumer goods make up a large share of non-energy imports under CPEC-linked industrial ties. This import structure is the core driver of Pakistan's recurring current-account and reserves pressure.", 'partners': "China is Pakistan's largest source of imports and its largest bilateral creditor (nearly $29bn in loans per World Bank data), while the United States remains the single largest destination for Pakistani textile exports. Gulf states -- Saudi Arabia and the UAE -- are critical not as trade partners but as sources of central-bank deposit rollovers and remittance inflows from millions of Pakistani expatriate workers. The EU is a significant textile export market under GSP+ preferential access, which Islamabad has repeatedly lobbied to preserve amid periodic human-rights-linked review threats."},
    'QAT': {'sectors': "Qatar's economy is dominated by liquefied natural gas, produced and marketed almost entirely through state-owned QatarEnergy, which operates the giant offshore North Field -- the world's largest non-associated gas field. The ongoing North Field East/South/West expansion is set to lift LNG capacity from 77 to 142 million tonnes per annum by 2030, underwriting continued fiscal surpluses and outbound investment via the Qatar Investment Authority (QIA). Non-hydrocarbon sectors -- financial services, real estate, and tourism/events infrastructure built out for the 2022 World Cup -- have grown but remain small relative to the roughly $220 billion in state-directed infrastructure investment over 2010-2022.", 'exports': "LNG and associated gas liquids account for the large majority of Qatar's exports, with QatarEnergy's international partners (TotalEnergies, Shell, ExxonMobil, ConocoPhillips, Eni, and Chinese state oil companies) holding minority equity stakes in expansion trains in exchange for long-term offtake agreements. Long-term LNG supply contracts with Asian buyers (Japan, South Korea, China, India) and European utilities (seeking to replace Russian pipeline gas since 2022) anchor Qatar's export revenue, supplemented by smaller volumes of crude oil, condensate, and petrochemicals. The North Field expansion is explicitly designed to defend Qatar's LNG market share against rising US and Australian export capacity.", 'imports': 'Qatar imports machinery, construction materials, foodstuffs, and consumer goods, with import needs and routes reshaped by the 2017-2021 Saudi-led blockade, during which imports fell roughly 40% before Doha diversified supply chains through Oman, Turkey, and direct sea routes. The blockade experience prompted lasting investment in domestic food security (dairy, poultry) and diversified shipping logistics via Hamad Port. Imports of capital goods and construction materials surged around the 2022 World Cup buildout and remain elevated for continued LNG-expansion-related engineering and construction contracts.', 'partners': "Asian LNG buyers -- Japan, South Korea, China, and increasingly India -- are Qatar's most important economic partners on the export side, while European utilities have become larger buyers since 2022. The United States is both a major security partner (hosting the Al Udeid Air Base) and, through ExxonMobil and ConocoPhillips, a key equity partner in North Field expansion trains. Qatar's relationship with fellow GCC states Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Bahrain, and Egypt was severely damaged by the 2017-2021 blockade and only partially normalized since the 2021 Al-Ula declaration, while Qatar's continued hosting of Hamas's political office and mediating role with Iran and the Taliban set it apart from other Gulf states' foreign postures."},
    'SAU': {'sectors': "Saudi Arabia's economy remains anchored in hydrocarbons, with state oil giant Saudi Aramco operating the world's largest conventional oil reserves and generating the bulk of government revenue; oil and gas exports accounted for about 73% of total merchandise exports in 2024. Vision 2030 has pushed diversification into petrochemicals (SABIC), mining (Ma'aden), tourism, entertainment, and giga-projects such as NEOM and Qiddiya, financed largely through the Public Investment Fund (PIF), which held about $1.21 trillion in assets at end-2025. Non-oil sectors such as construction, financial services, and chemicals have grown -- non-oil exports rose 13.1% in 2024 -- but the fiscal and external accounts remain highly sensitive to oil-price swings, as shown by the 2020 and 2025-26 deficit episodes.", 'exports': "Oil exports accounted for about 73% of Saudi Arabia's total merchandise exports in 2024, while non-oil exports (including re-exports) grew 13.1% that year to help push total trade past SR2 trillion; chemical industry products were the top non-oil export category at 25.5% of the non-oil total. China was the single largest destination, taking about 15.2% of total Saudi exports in 2024, reflecting Riyadh's deepening reliance on Asian energy demand. Aramco and SABIC remain the dominant exporting entities, supplemented by growing petrochemical and mineral shipments tied to Vision 2030 diversification.", 'imports': "Saudi Arabia imports machinery, transport equipment, foodstuffs, chemicals, and metals to support both consumption and the construction boom tied to giga-projects like NEOM, Qiddiya, and the Red Sea developments. China supplied about 23.9% of Saudi Arabia's total imports in 2024, making it the kingdom's largest source of imports as well as its top export destination. Import demand has also been driven by materials and contracting services for PIF-backed megaprojects, a channel through which Vision 2030 spending translates into current-account outflows.", 'partners': "China is Saudi Arabia's single largest trading partner on both the export and import side, a relationship deepened by Aramco's downstream investments in Chinese refining and petrochemicals. The United States remains the kingdom's most important security and defense partner and a key destination for PIF portfolio investment, even as bilateral trade in goods has receded in relative terms. Within the GCC, the UAE and Bahrain are close economic partners, reinforced by the 2021 Al-Ula reconciliation that also restored full economic ties with Qatar; India and South Korea are growing markets for petrochemicals."},
    'SYR': {'sectors': "Thirteen years of war have devastated Syria's productive base: manufacturing, agriculture and oil sectors that once made Syria a lower-middle-income, near self-sufficient economy have been reduced to a fraction of their former size, with roughly a third of pre-war capital stock destroyed according to the World Bank's October 2025 assessment. Agriculture (especially wheat and cotton in the north and east) and a small revival in oil and gas production in government-recovered fields are the leading real sectors under the transitional government, alongside a construction sector newly energized by reconstruction demand. The economy remains overwhelmingly informal and cash-based, with an estimated 90% of the population below the poverty line as of 2024-2025.", 'exports': 'Pre-war Syria exported oil, cotton textiles, phosphates and processed foods, but oil exports collapsed after 2012 when the EU and US sanctioned Syrian crude and opposition/Kurdish forces seized key eastern oil fields. Remaining exports are dominated by olive oil, other agricultural produce and limited refined products, shipped mainly to Lebanon, Iraq and Gulf states; the transitional government has prioritized restoring oil and gas output as a re-export earner, cited by the IMF as a driver of projected 10%-plus 2026 growth. A return of Gulf capital following 2025 sanctions relief is expected to gradually restore export capacity, though pre-2011 volumes remain a distant benchmark.', 'imports': "Syria imports the great majority of its fuel, wheat, medicine and manufactured goods, a dependence that deepened as domestic refining and agriculture were devastated by war; Iran supplied oil and credit lines to the Assad government for years under sanctions, while Russia provided wheat and military-linked goods. Since Assad's fall, Gulf states -- Saudi Arabia, Qatar and the UAE -- have stepped in to fund fuel shipments and electricity supply for the transitional government, alongside Turkish construction materials and consumer goods flowing across the northern border. The lifting of most US and EU sanctions in mid-to-late 2025 is expected to reopen formal import-finance channels previously routed through informal and sanctioned networks for over a decade.", 'partners': "Turkey and the Gulf states (Qatar, Saudi Arabia, UAE) have emerged as Syria's most important economic partners under the transitional government, financing public salaries, energy imports and reconstruction pledges since Assad's fall, while Iran and Russia -- the previous government's principal military and financial backers -- have been largely sidelined, with Damascus now auditing the legality of prior Iranian loan spending. The US and EU, having lifted the bulk of their sanctions in 2025, are re-emerging as normalization partners, and the World Bank and IMF resumed direct engagement with Damascus in 2025 for the first time in over a decade. China remains a marginal but growing trade partner, mostly for manufactured imports."},
    'TUN': {'sectors': "Tunisia's economy rests on tourism (over 7% of GDP directly, ~15% including indirect effects), phosphate mining and processing centered on the Gafsa Basin (which accounts for over 90% of national phosphate rock output and roughly 4% of GDP), textiles and garment manufacturing, olive oil production, and agriculture more broadly. State-owned enterprises dominate large swaths of the non-tourism economy and carry substantial debt, a structural weakness that has been central to (and a sticking point in) stalled IMF negotiations since 2022-2023.", 'exports': "Tunisia is the world's second-largest olive oil exporter after Spain, with exports surging 55% to a record 368,000 tonnes and $1.6bn in revenue in the first nine months of the 2025/26 season amid favorable harvests and global prices; textiles and apparel exports to the EU reached €2.5bn in 2024, with Tunisia the EU's top supplier of work clothing (17.44% share) and fourth-largest jeans supplier. Phosphate and derivative exports (via state firm Compagnie des Phosphates de Gafsa) contribute roughly 15% of exports, though output remains well below pre-2011 levels due to periodic social unrest and infrastructure decay in mining regions.", 'imports': "Tunisia imports energy products, machinery, and raw materials for its textile and manufacturing sectors, with import costs increasingly difficult to finance given the government's loss of access to Eurobond markets since 2020-2021 (following Moody's and Fitch downgrades to deep junk/CCC- territory) and its 2023 rejection of a stalled IMF program. Since 2024, the Central Bank of Tunisia has been authorized to directly finance up to a combined $4.45bn (2024-2025) of treasury needs including import-related debt service, a workaround for lost external market access.", 'partners': "The EU is overwhelmingly Tunisia's largest trade partner, accounting for 59.5% of total 2025 trade (73.2% of exports, 49.6% of imports), making Brussels both Tunisia's key commercial partner and, via the 2023 Memorandum of Understanding (~€1bn for migration, budget, and energy support led by Italy and the Netherlands), a substitute source of budget support since IMF talks collapsed. Absent IMF and Eurobond financing, Tunisia has leaned on domestic central-bank financing and reduced its foreign-debt share of the budget from 73.8% (2018) to 57.8% (2024) — a policy President Kais Saied frames as sovereignty from Western 'diktats' but which rating agencies view as a sign of shrinking external-financing options."},
    'YEM': {'sectors': "Yemen's pre-war economy relied on hydrocarbons for roughly 80% of government revenue and 25-30% of GDP, anchored by crude oil exports and the Yemen LNG project at Balhaf (commissioned 2009, backed by $4-5bn in investment), but war has devastated this base: government-controlled oil production has been repeatedly shut down by Houthi attacks on export terminals, most severely from late 2022. Agriculture (including qat, a mildly narcotic leaf crop occupying a disproportionate share of scarce water and arable land), artisanal fishing, and a small remittance-dependent services sector are the other pillars, alongside significant Gulf and UN humanitarian financing that now substitutes for a large share of formal economic activity. The country's two rival administrations -- the Houthi-controlled north and the internationally recognized government based in Aden -- each run separate, poorly integrated fiscal and monetary systems.", 'exports': "Yemen's exports are dominated by crude oil and, when operational, liquefied natural gas from the Balhaf LNG facility, alongside smaller volumes of fish, coffee (a heritage crop attracting renewed global interest) and honey. Government-controlled oil exports through terminals at Ash Shihr and Dhabba have been intermittently halted by Houthi drone and missile strikes, most significantly from October 2022, eliminating the internationally recognized government's main hard-currency earner for extended periods. Export destinations have historically included China, the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Oman and Malaysia, though volumes remain far below pre-2015 levels.", 'imports': "Yemen imports the overwhelming majority of its food -- more than 90% of staples including wheat, rice and sugar -- along with refined fuel products, machinery and pharmaceuticals, making it acutely exposed to global food-price shocks and disruption of its own ports. Imports flow mainly through Hodeidah (Houthi-controlled, on the Red Sea) and Aden (government-controlled), both of which have seen periodic blockade-like restrictions and, since late 2023, reduced shipping traffic amid Houthi attacks on Red Sea vessels that cut Hodeidah's throughput by roughly 15%. Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Turkey and India are leading suppliers of food and consumer goods.", 'partners': "Saudi Arabia and the UAE are Yemen's most consequential external actors, both as members of the military coalition backing the internationally recognized government and as the leading providers of budget support, fuel-purchase financing and reconstruction aid, including periodic direct cash deposits to shore up the rial. China and India are significant trade partners for both oil purchases and consumer-goods imports, while Iran is the Houthi movement's principal external patron, providing weapons and political support that have entrenched the north-south economic and territorial split. The World Bank's IDA and the UN's humanitarian architecture function as de facto financiers of last resort for a state that has had no functioning unified fiscal system since 2014."},
}


